<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-student-guide-short-answer-analysis"
         xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analyzing Student Mathematical Reasoning</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Students responses to short-answer questions may be only one or two 
      sentences in length, but they can still show what information a student 
      noticed, how the student connected that information to a mathematical 
      idea, and what conclusion the student reached.
    </p>

    <p>
      This project studies those short explanations. Our goal is to examine the
      mathematical justification contained in the response. In particular, we
      ask:
      <ul>
        <li>What feature or fact did the student notice?</li>
        <li>What conclusion did the student draw?</li>
        <li>How did the student connect the feature to the conclusion?</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      This guide outlines the basic ideas of our approach.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Explain the purpose of the project.
      </li>
      <li>
        Identify the criterion, inferential link, and judgment in a short
        mathematical explanation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Describe how a response moves through the analysis pipeline.
      </li>
      <li>
        Recognize decisions that require interpretation by a human researcher.
      </li>
      <li>
        Annotate introductory examples using the Episode Model.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 1: BALACHEFF FRAMEWORK                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-balacheff">
    <title>The Balacheff Framework</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This project is motivated by the cK&#162; framework developed by
        Balacheff and his collaborators (Balacheff &amp; Gaudin, 2009). The
        framework provides a language for describing mathematical knowing as it
        appears when a person works on a particular kind of problem.
      </p>

      <p>
        A central idea is that researchers should be cautious when moving from
        something a student writes or does to a claim about what the student
        knows. A written response provides evidence, but it is not a direct view
        into the student's mind. The framework therefore directs attention to the
        problem being solved, the mathematical actions taken, the representations
        used, and the means by which the student appears to make decisions or
        judge a result.
      </p>

      <p>
        In this framework, a conception is situated: it describes a form of
        mathematical knowing that functions within a particular class of
        problems and representations. It should not be interpreted as a fixed
        belief or permanent characteristic located entirely within the student.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-conception-model">
      <title>The Model of a Conception</title>

      <p>
        According to Balacheff and Gaudin, a conception is modeled by the
        quadruplet
        <md>
          (P,R,L,\Sigma),
        </md>
        whose four components describe different parts of the mathematical
        activity.

        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>Problems</title>
            <p>
              <m>P</m> is the class of problems or situations that the conception can
              successfully address.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Operators</title>
            <p>
              <m>R</m> is the set of mathematical actions used to work on a problem 
              or transform a representation (from <m>L</m>). Examples include row 
              reducing a matrix, substituting into an equation, differentiating a 
              function, or rearranging a symbolic expression.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Representation System</title>
            <p>
              <m>L</m> is the linguistic, symbolic, graphical, or other means that
              mathematical work and feedback are expressed. Examples include
              equations, matrices, diagrams, written mathematical language, and
              software interfaces.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Control Structure</title>
            <p>
              <m>\Sigma</m> is the means used to make choices, evaluate actions, 
              validate results, and decide whether a problem has been solved. 
              Examples include using a definition, checking a computed result, 
              recognizing that a system is consistent, or using the presence of a
              free variable to classify how many solutions a system has.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice that <m>R</m> denotes <em>operators</em>, not representations.
        Balacheff and Gaudin use <m>L</m> for the representation system.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-control-structures">
      <title>Control Structures</title>

      <p>
        Our project is especially interested in the evidence for control
        structures found in students' written justifications. Short-answer
        responses often do not show every mathematical operation a student
        performed, but they may state the feature the student noticed and the
        conclusion the student drew from it.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example, a student might use the presence of a free variable, the
        number of pivots, the consistency of a system, or the sign of a
        derivative to justify a conclusion. These features function as
        <term>criteria</term> within the student's reasoning.
      </p>

      <p>
        A criterion and a control structure are related, but they are not
        identical. A criterion is a particular condition or feature recognized
        in a response. A control structure is the broader means of using such
        information to make a decision or validate a conclusion. The
        <term>Episode Model</term>, introduced later in this guide, records how a
        criterion is connected to a judgment in a particular response.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-balacheff-limits">
      <title>What We Can and Cannot Claim</title>

      <p>
        A short response may provide evidence that a student used a particular
        mathematical criterion or enacted a particular control structure. It
        does not ordinarily provide enough evidence to reconstruct an entire
        conception, including its full range of problems, operators,
        representations, and controls.
      </p>

      <p>
        For this reason, the project uses language such as:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <q>the response provides evidence for a control structure,</q>
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>the student appears to use the following criterion,</q> or
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>the written justification contains the following episode.</q>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The project avoids statements such as:
        <ul>
          <li><q>the tool detected the student's conception,</q></li>
          <li><q>the student believes this in every situation,</q> or</li>
          <li>
            <q>the response gives a complete model of the student's thinking.</q>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This distinction is important. The framework motivates the categories
        used in the analysis, but the project analyzes evidence contained in
        written responses rather than claiming to model students' complete
        conceptions.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 2: PROJECT DESCRIPTION                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-project">
    <title>Project Description</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Our goal is to develop a computational tool for analyzing short-answer 
        responses to mathematical questions. The project uses real student
        responses collected from interactive textbooks hosted on the Runestone
        platform.
      </p>

      <p>
        The tool is intended to assist researchers, not replace them. It helps
        organize responses, identify possible pieces of reasoning, compare those
        pieces with a question-specific codebook, and direct difficult cases to
        human reviewers.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project has three closely related goals:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Analytic goal.</term>
              Develop a clear and consistent method for describing the
              justifications found in short student responses.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Computational goal.</term>
              Build a pipeline that can assist with screening, extracting,
              matching, and organizing large collections of responses.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Research goal.</term>
              Study whether this process improves the reliability, efficiency,
              and transparency of response coding.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        The main object analyzed by the project is an
        <term>episode of justification</term>. An episode records a local piece
        of reasoning visible in a response. It does not claim to capture
        everything the student knew or thought.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-project-data">
      <title>What the Project Studies</title>

      <p>
        The basic unit of data is a student's written response to a specific
        mathematical question. To interpret a response responsibly, we consider:
        <ul>
          <li>the exact question the student was answering;</li>
          <li>the mathematical information supplied in the question;</li>
          <li>the conclusion or explanation the question requested; and</li>
          <li> the definitions, theorems, examples, etc. available to the student.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The meaning and correctness of a statement often depend on the problem in
        which it appears. For example, the existence of a free variable may
        justify one conclusion when a system is known to be consistent, but it
        may not justify the same conclusion when consistency has not been
        established.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-project-codebook">
      <title>The Role of the Codebook</title>

      <p>
        A <term>codebook</term> is a structured guide for classifying the
        mathematical reasoning found in responses. It contains named codes,
        definitions, inclusion and exclusion rules, and examples.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some codes describe broad control structures that may appear in several
        questions. Examples include reasoning based on pivots, free variables,
        consistency, dimension, or the sign of a derivative. However, the way a
        control structure is used must still be interpreted within the
        particular question.
      </p>

      <p>
        For this reason, the project also uses
        <term>question-specific coding information</term>. For a given question,
        researchers identify:
        <ul>
          <li>the mathematical criteria that may be relevant;</li>
          <li>the judgments a student might reasonably draw;</li>
          <li>
            the inferential links that validly connect those criteria and
            judgments; and
          </li>
          <li>
            common incorrect, incomplete, or ambiguous uses of the same ideas.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The codebook does not remove the need for interpretation. It gives
        researchers a common language and a shared set of rules so that their
        interpretations can be compared, discussed, and revised.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two structural facts about the codebook are worth knowing early. First,
        the named control structures live in a single shared library that is
        reused across questions. Each question then has a <term>working
        codebook</term>: the controls that apply to it, each paired with the
        criterion-to-judgment relationship that is valid <em>for that
        question</em>. Correctness is judged against that per-question
        relationship, not against a global assumption. Second, new terms and
        codes are not added casually. A proposed term or code enters in a
        <term>proposed</term> state, and a human reviewer either accepts it as
        new or maps it to an existing entry; an entry that has already been used
        in coding is <term>deprecated</term> rather than deleted, so earlier
        analyses stay interpretable.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-project-tool">
      <title>What the Tool Does</title>

      <p>
        The computational tool processes responses through a sequence of small
        tasks. At different points, it may:
        <ul>
          <li>
            organize responses and preserve information about their source;
          </li>
          <li>
            identify responses that contain enough justification to analyze;
          </li>
          <li>
            propose one or more episodes found in a response;
          </li>
          <li>
            compare those episodes with the current codebook;
          </li>
          <li>
            identify possible codes rather than forcing a single answer;
          </li>
          <li>
            flag incomplete, ambiguous, contradictory, or unfamiliar reasoning;
            and
          </li>
          <li>
            direct the response to an appropriate form of human review.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The tool also preserves an <term>audit trail</term>. This means that a
        researcher should be able to determine:
        <ul>
          <li>which original response was analyzed;</li>
          <li>which version of the codebook was used;</li>
          <li>what interpretation the tool proposed;</li>
          <li>why the response was flagged or assigned a candidate code;</li>
          <li>what a human reviewer changed; and</li>
          <li>which version of the tool produced the result.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This record is important because the project is not only interested in
        the final codes. It is also interested in how those codes were produced
        and where uncertainty or disagreement occurred.
      </p>

      <aside>
        <title>Tool Capabilities</title>
        <p>
          Currently, every stage runs a simple <term>deterministic baseline</term>,
          not a large language model. For a list of the tool's current capabilities, 
          see <xref ref="sec-student-guide-pipeline" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-project-human-role">
      <title>What Human Researchers Decide</title>

      <p>
        Human researchers remain responsible for the mathematical and
        interpretive decisions in the project. In particular, researchers:
        <ul>
          <li>
            determine the mathematical goal and reference solution for each
            question;
          </li>
          <li>
            develop, test, and revise the codebook;
          </li>
          <li>
            decide how much reasoning must be present for a response to be
            codable;
          </li>
          <li>
            determine how a response should be divided into episodes;
          </li>
          <li>
            decide whether a proposed code accurately represents the response;
          </li>
          <li>
            evaluate whether a criterion is true and an inferential link is
            valid;
          </li>
          <li>
            resolve disagreements and ambiguous cases; and
          </li>
          <li>
            decide whether an unfamiliar response requires a new code or a
            revision to an existing definition.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The tool may propose an interpretation, but the proposal is not
        automatically treated as a research finding. A human reviewer may
        confirm it, revise it, reject it, or mark the response as unresolved.
      </p>

      <p>
        Uncertainty and disagreement are not treated only as problems to be
        removed. They may reveal that:
        <ul>
          <li>the response is genuinely ambiguous;</li>
          <li>the codebook definition is unclear or overlapping;</li>
          <li>the controlled vocabulary is incomplete;</li>
          <li>the tool has made an error; or</li>
          <li>
            two researchers are applying the analytic framework differently.
          </li>
        </ul>
        These cases provide useful evidence for improving both the framework and
        the tool.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-project-research-questions">
      <title>Questions the Project Investigates</title>

      <p>
        Some project questions concern the mathematical reasoning visible in
        student responses:
        <ul>
          <li>
            What mathematical criteria appear in students' written
            justifications?
          </li>
          <li>
            How do students connect those criteria to their conclusions?
          </li>
          <li>
            Which control structures occur across several questions or topics?
          </li>
          <li>
            When do students cite a correct criterion but draw an invalid
            conclusion?
          </li>
          <li>
            When does a response contain sound local reasoning but fail to answer
            the complete question?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Other questions concern the quality of the analytic process:
        <ul>
          <li>
            Which types of responses are difficult for human coders to classify
            consistently?
          </li>
          <li>
            Which responses can be processed reliably by a computational tool,
            and which require human interpretation?
          </li>
          <li>
            How closely do tool-supported classifications agree with independent
            human coding?
          </li>
          <li>
            Does the tool reduce or reorganize the amount of work required from
            researchers?
          </li>
          <li>
            What can disagreements among coders, tools, and codebooks teach us
            about the analytic method?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The long-term aim is not merely to produce more codes more quickly. It is
        to develop a process that is mathematically meaningful, consistent,
        inspectable, and useful for studying large collections of student
        responses.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 3: ANATOMY OF AN EPISODE                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-episode">
    <title>The Anatomy of an Episode</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A student response may contain one or more local pieces of
        justification. The project represents each such piece as an
        <term>episode</term>.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a response <m>x</m>, the extracted sequence of episodes is written
        <md>
          E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_n\rangle.
        </md>
        The angle brackets indicate that the episodes are recorded in an order,
        usually following the order in which the reasoning develops.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each episode has the form
        <md>
          e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k),
        </md>
        where:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>C_k</m> is the <term>criterion</term>: an observation, fact,
            condition, intermediate result, or mathematical feature cited by the
            student;
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>I_k</m> is the <term>inferential link</term>: the reasoning rule
            or mathematical relationship used to connect the criterion to a
            conclusion; and
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>J_k</m> is the <term>judgment</term>: the conclusion or decision
            the student draws.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A useful first picture is:
        <md>
          \text{criterion}
          \quad \xrightarrow{\text{inferential link}} \quad
          \text{judgment}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        For example, consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        There is a free variable, so the solution cannot be unique.
      </program>

      <p>
        One episode can be represented as:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>
            <p>A free variable exists.</p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>
            <p>
              A system with a free variable cannot have exactly one solution.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>
            <p>The solution is not unique.</p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The episode records the local justification visible in the response. It
        does not claim to describe everything the student knew, every operation
        the student performed, or every thought that led to the written answer.
      </p>

		<p>
			Complete episodes receive a two-part correctness code, adapted from
      prior work (Gerami et al., 2024):
			<md>
				\operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c^1_k,c^2_k).
			</md>
			The first digit <m>c^1_k</m> records whether the cited criterion is true of
      the adopted reference state. The second digit <m>c^2_k</m> records whether
      the inference from criterion to judgment is mathematically valid for the
      question. Incomplete episodes are not forced into this two-digit scheme; they
      are preserved as partial evidence or routed to human review.
		</p>

    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-episode-project-model">
      <title>The Episode Model Is a Project Analytic Tool</title>

      <p>
        The Episode Model is motivated by the cK&#162; framework, especially its
        attention to control structures and validation. However, the tuple
        <m>(C,I,J)</m> is not itself part of Balacheff's original model of a
        conception.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project introduces episodes as a practical way to analyze short
        written responses. The cK&#162; framework provides the theoretical
        vocabulary, while the Episode Model provides the smaller unit that human
        coders and the computational tool use when examining a response.
      </p>

      <p>
        This distinction matters because a conception is represented by the much
        broader quadruplet <m>(P,R,L,\Sigma)</m>. An episode captures only a
        local trace of justification:
        <ul>
          <li>a criterion the student appears to use;</li>
          <li>a connection from that criterion to a judgment; and</li>
          <li>the resulting judgment.</li>
        </ul>
        It therefore provides evidence related to a control structure without
        being treated as a complete conception.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-episode-evidence">
      <title>Finding Evidence for the Three Parts</title>

      <p>
        The three components of an episode are not always written as three
        separate sentences. A criterion, link, and judgment may all appear in a
        single sentence, or one component may be expressed only through the
        structure of the response.
      </p>

      <p>
        Words such as <q>because</q>, <q>since</q>, <q>so</q>,
        <q>therefore</q>, and <q>which means</q> often help identify how a
        student connects ideas. For example:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The system is inconsistent, so the vector is not in the span.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Here, the word <q>so</q> helps identify:
        <ul>
          <li>
            the criterion: the corresponding system is inconsistent;
          </li>
          <li>
            the inferential link: an inconsistent system has no solution, so the
            target vector cannot be written as a linear combination of the given
            vectors; and
          </li>
          <li>
            the judgment: the vector is not in the span.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Connector words are clues, not guarantees. A response may contain the
        word <q>because</q> but still fail to state a meaningful mathematical
        connection. Conversely, a student may express a clear inference without
        using an explicit connector.
      </p>

      <p>
        When reading a response, a coder should distinguish among three levels
        of evidence:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Explicit</title>
            <p>
              The component is directly stated in the student's words or
              symbols.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Strongly supported</title>
            <p>
              The component is not stated word for word, but the structure and
              mathematical content of the response identify it without requiring
              the coder to add new reasoning.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Missing or ambiguous</title>
            <p>
              Identifying the component would require the coder to guess,
              complete, or repair the student's reasoning.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal is to represent the justification the student provided, not the
        stronger justification the student could have provided.
      </p>

      <p>
        When you record the inferential link on a coding sheet, these three
        levels map onto the values the <term>Coder's Handbook</term> uses: an
        explicit connector is <c>explicit</c>, a strongly supported but unstated
        link is <c>implicit</c>, and a missing or unidentifiable link is
        <c>absent</c> (which marks the episode incomplete).
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-complete-episode">
      <title>Complete and Incomplete Episodes</title>

      <p>
        A <term>complete episode</term> contains enough evidence to identify a
        criterion, an inferential link, and a judgment. The link need not be
        written as a formal theorem, but the response must provide enough
        information to determine how the criterion is being used to support the
        judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        An <term>incomplete episode</term> is missing one or more of these
        parts. For example, consider:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There is a free variable.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This response identifies a criterion, but it does not state what
        conclusion the student draws from that criterion. Depending on the
        question, a reviewer may recognize several possible continuations:
        <ul>
          <li>the solution is not unique;</li>
          <li>the system has infinitely many solutions;</li>
          <li>a particular variable is free; or</li>
          <li>the columns do not satisfy a requested condition.</li>
        </ul>
        The coder must not choose one of these conclusions for the student.
      </p>

      <p>
        Missing information is preserved as missing information. An incomplete
        episode may still be useful as partial evidence, but it is not forced
        into the same correctness coding used for complete episodes. It is
        normally flagged for human review.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-multiple-episodes">
      <title>Responses with More Than One Episode</title>

      <p>
        A response may contain several episodes. The first task is to determine
        where one local justification ends and another begins.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There are fewer pivots than variables, so there is a free variable.
          Therefore, the solution cannot be unique.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This response contains two linked episodes:
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
            e_1 \amp: \text{fewer pivots than variables}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{a free variable exists},\\
            e_2 \amp: \text{a free variable exists}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{the solution is not unique}.
          \end{aligned}
        </md>
        The judgment of <m>e_1</m> becomes the criterion of <m>e_2</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Multiple episodes may relate to one another in several ways:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Chained episodes</title>
            <p>
              The judgment of one episode becomes the criterion for a later
              episode. Together, the episodes form a sequence of reasoning.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Independent episodes</title>
            <p>
              The student supplies separate justifications that do not depend on
              one another. For example, the student might justify the same answer
              using both a pivot argument and a dimension argument.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Nested episodes</title>
            <p>
              One episode functions as a sub-step inside a larger
              justification. A nested episode may also be part of a chain, so
              these descriptions are not always mutually exclusive.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Nested episodes create a coding decision. Depending on the project's
        approved policy, researchers might code both the larger episode and its
        sub-step, only the larger episode, or only the most specific sub-step.
        This is a coding convention that the research team must apply
        consistently; the mathematics alone does not determine which policy to
        use.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-operation-control">
      <title>Operations and Controls</title>

      <p>
        The project distinguishes between an <term>operation</term> and a
        <term>control</term>.
      </p>

      <p>
        An operation is generative: it creates or transforms a mathematical
        object. Examples include:
        <ul>
          <li>row reducing a matrix;</li>
          <li>differentiating a function;</li>
          <li>substituting a value into an equation;</li>
          <li>solving a system; or</li>
          <li>constructing a parametric solution.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A control is evaluative: it reads or interprets a feature of the current
        mathematical state and supports a decision. Examples include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            recognizing that a system is inconsistent because its reduced matrix
            contains a contradictory row;
          </li>
          <li>
            deciding that a solution is not unique because a free variable
            exists; or
          </li>
          <li>
            concluding that a function is increasing because its derivative is
            positive.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The distinction can be illustrated by the following two statements:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Operation</title>
            <p><q>I row reduced the augmented matrix.</q></p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Control</title>
            <p>
              <q>The reduced matrix has no contradictory row, so the system is
              consistent.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The first statement describes an action. The second evaluates the result
        of that action and draws a judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        An episode records an enacted control rather than the operation by
        itself. Operations may still be recorded as useful context, especially
        when they produce the criterion used in an episode.
      </p>

      <p>
        This distinction is a coding convention adopted by the project, and the
        boundary is not always perfectly sharp. A theorem, calculation, or
        intermediate result can sometimes function as an operation in one part
        of a response and as a control in another. Ambiguous cases should be
        documented and discussed rather than resolved by an unsupported guess.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-local-global">
      <title>Local Justification and the Complete Answer</title>

      <p>
        Episodes are local units of reasoning. A well-formed and mathematically
        valid episode does not necessarily mean that the student fully answered
        the question.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider a question that asks a student to decide whether a system has a
        unique solution and to identify its basic and free variables. A student
        might write:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There are two pivots, so one variable is free.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This may contain a sound local episode:
        <md>
          \text{two pivots among three variables}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{one variable is free}.
        </md>
        However, the student has not yet stated whether the solution is unique
        or identified which variable is free. The episode may be sound even
        though the response does not cover the complete goal of the question.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project therefore distinguishes:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Justification quality</title>
            <p>
              Whether the criterion is mathematically accurate and whether the
              inferential link validly supports the judgment.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Goal coverage</title>
            <p>
              Whether the response reaches all conclusions or completes all
              tasks requested by the question.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        These questions must be evaluated separately. A response may contain
        sound reasoning but be incomplete, or it may reach the requested final
        answer through an invalid or unsupported inference.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 4: PIPELINE FLOW                                     -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-pipeline">
    <title>The Pipeline Flow</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The computational tool processes student responses through a sequence of
        small stages called a <term>pipeline</term>. Each stage performs a limited
        task, records its result, and passes structured information to the next
        stage.
      </p>

      <p>
        The short-answer pipeline is summarized by:
        <md>
          \text{ingest}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{screen}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{extract}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{match}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{dispose}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Dividing the analysis into stages makes the process easier to inspect.
        When a researcher disagrees with a final result, the researcher can ask:
        <ul>
          <li>Was the original response imported correctly?</li>
          <li>Was the response screened incorrectly?</li>
          <li>Was its reasoning divided into the wrong episodes?</li>
          <li>Did an episode receive an inappropriate candidate code?</li>
          <li>Was the response sent to the wrong kind of review?</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Human review is not merely a sixth stage placed at the end of the
        pipeline. Human judgment surrounds the entire process. Researchers define
        the coding rules, inspect uncertain cases, correct proposed
        interpretations, and revise the codebook when recurring disagreements
        reveal a problem.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-pipeline-overview">
      <title>Overview of the Five Stages</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-pipeline-overview">
        <title>The purpose and principal output of each pipeline stage.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="15%"/>
          <col width="43%"/>
          <col width="42%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Stage</cell>
            <cell>Main question</cell>
            <cell>Principal output</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Ingest</cell>
            <cell>
              What response was submitted, to which question, and from which
              source?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              A standardized response record with source information.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Screen</cell>
            <cell>
              Does the response contain enough interpretable justification to
              analyze?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              A screening decision, rationale, and possible review flag.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Extract</cell>
            <cell>
              What local episodes of justification are visible in the response?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              One or more proposed episodes, including missing or uncertain
              components.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Match</cell>
            <cell>
              Which codebook entries could describe each extracted episode?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Zero, one, or several candidate codes with rationales.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Dispose</cell>
            <cell>
              What should happen to the response next?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              A workflow disposition: <c>auto_accept</c>,
              <c>standard_review</c>, <c>terminology_flag</c>, <c>no_match</c>,
              or <c>escalate</c> (a sixth value, <c>problem_type_flag</c>, is
              defined but not yet produced).
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Two points keep this overview honest. First, the same five stages are
        named for their outputs here (ingest, screen, extract, match, dispose)
        and for their agents in the <xref ref="ch-automation-tool-roadmap" text="title"/>
        (Ingestor, Screener, Extractor, Matcher, Disposer); they are the same
        stages. Second, what runs today is not a language model. Every stage
        currently uses a <term>deterministic baseline</term>: a rule-based
        screener, a keyword-and-connector extractor, a substring pattern
        matcher, and a rule-cascade disposer. These baselines let the pipeline
        run end to end right now, but their proposals are deliberately shallow.
        A language model is planned for only the screening and extraction
        stages, is to be introduced screening-stage-first, and is awaiting IRB 
        approval.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-pipeline-general">
      <title>General Pipeline Pseudocode</title>

      <p>
        The following pseudocode describes the overall logical flow. It is not
        programming code. It is a plain-language description of the decisions
        the pipeline makes.
      </p>

      <program language="text">
  For each student response:

      preserve the original response and its source

      screen the response for interpretable justification

      if the response should continue:
          extract each local episode
          record its criterion, inferential link, and judgment
          preserve missing, ambiguous, and unfamiliar information
          compare each episode with the question-specific codebook
          record every plausible candidate code

      use the accumulated results to assign a workflow disposition

      present the original response, proposed analysis, and rationales
      to a human reviewer when required

      preserve any human confirmation, revision, rejection,
      or unresolved decision
      </program>

      <p>
        Notice that the pipeline preserves both the original response and the
        intermediate results. A later stage does not erase what an earlier stage
        produced.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-stage-ingest">
      <title>Stage 1: Ingest</title>

      <p>
        The ingest stage reads responses from a source file and converts them
        into a consistent format. Source files may come from different systems
        and may use different column names, file formats, or conventions.
      </p>

      <p>
        This stage answers basic organizational questions:
        <ul>
          <li>What did the student write?</li>
          <li>Which question was the student answering?</li>
          <li>Which student, course, section, or semester produced the response?</li>
          <li>When and from which source file was the response imported?</li>
          <li>Has the same response already been imported?</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Ingest does not interpret the mathematical reasoning. It should not
        decide whether a response is correct, codable, or connected to a
        particular control structure.
      </p>

      <p>
        A successful ingest produces a stable response record that every later
        stage can reference.
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Stage 1 pseudocode</title>

        <program language="text">
          For each row in the source file:

              determine whether the row represents a student response

              if the row is a response:
                  read the response text
                  read the student and question identifiers
                  preserve available source information
                  create a stable response identifier

                  if the same response is not already stored:
                      save the standardized response record
                  otherwise:
                      record it as a duplicate and do not create a second copy

              if the row cannot be interpreted:
                  preserve a warning explaining why it was skipped
        </program>
      </paragraphs>

      <p>
        The original wording should be preserved as faithfully as possible.
        Spelling errors, unusual notation, incomplete sentences, and informal
        language may all be analytically important and should not be silently
        corrected during ingest.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-stage-screen">
      <title>Stage 2: Screen</title>

      <p>
        The screening stage asks whether a response contains enough
        interpretable justification to continue through the analytic pipeline.
        Screening is not the same as grading. A mathematically incorrect response
        may still contain clear and codable reasoning.
      </p>

      <p>
        A response may be excluded from further automatic analysis when it is:
        <ul>
          <li>blank;</li>
          <li>illegible or unintelligible;</li>
          <li>unrelated to the question;</li>
          <li>only a bare answer such as <q>yes</q> or <q>no</q>;</li>
          <li>
            mathematical work with no identifiable connection to a conclusion;
            or
          </li>
          <li>
            too ambiguous to interpret without adding reasoning not supplied by
            the student.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Borderline cases are preserved for human review rather than silently
        discarded. The current screening stage uses three labels: <c>INCLUDE</c>,
        <c>EXCLUDE</c>, and <c>FLAG</c>. The <c>FLAG</c> label is used for ambiguous responses: for example, one that shows mathematical work but no connector
        linking it to a conclusion, or one of meaningful length that never states an
        explicit connector. A <c>FLAG</c> is not a rejection; it reserves the response 
        for a human to make the final call. Each screening decision also carries a 
        confidence value, which can further prioritize a response for review.
      </p>

      <p>
        The screening result should include a short rationale. A label alone does
        not explain what feature of the response caused the decision.
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Stage 2 pseudocode</title>

        <program language="text">
          For each stored response:

              read the question and the response text

              if the response is blank:
                  propose EXCLUDE
                  record "blank response" as the rationale

              else if the response is unrelated or unintelligible:
                  propose EXCLUDE
                  record the specific reason

              else if the response gives only an answer with no justification:
                  propose EXCLUDE or FLAG
                  follow the current screening policy

              else if the response shows work but no connector to a conclusion,
                      or is otherwise borderline:
                  propose FLAG
                  reserve the response for human review
                  explain the uncertainty

              else:
                  propose INCLUDE
                  identify the evidence that makes the response analyzable

              record the screening decision (INCLUDE, EXCLUDE, or FLAG),
              its rationale, and its confidence
        </program>
      </paragraphs>

      <p>
        A response labeled <c>EXCLUDE</c> is not deleted. It remains part of the
        dataset, together with the reason for its exclusion. The distinction
        between codable and non-codable responses may itself be important in
        later research.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-stage-extract">
      <title>Stage 3: Extract</title>

      <p>
        The extraction stage converts a free-text response into one or more
        proposed episodes of justification. Its purpose is to make the local
        reasoning explicit without changing the mathematical meaning of the
        student's response.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ideally, for each possible episode the analysis identifies:
        <ul>
          <li>the criterion <m>C_k</m>;</li>
          <li>the judgment <m>J_k</m>;</li>
          <li>
            the order of the episodes, and whether they are chained,
            independent, or potentially nested; and
          </li>
          <li>
            whether any component is missing, ambiguous, or outside the
            controlled vocabulary.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The inferential link <m>I_k</m> is treated a little differently from the
        criterion and judgment. The extractor records the criterion and judgment
        a student states, but the <em>validity</em> of the link between them is
        not read off the student's words; it is evaluated later, at matching and
        correctness, against the question-specific coding information (the valid
        criterion-to-judgment relationships for that question). Making the
        criterion and judgment explicit is the extractor's job; supplying or
        certifying the link is not.
      </p>

      <p>
        The extractor should not repair the response. If a student states a
        criterion but does not provide a judgment, the output should preserve an
        incomplete episode rather than supplying the most likely conclusion.
      </p>

      <p>
        The extractor may normalize different expressions that have the same
        mathematical meaning. For example, <q>one variable can be anything</q>,
        <q>there is a free variable</q>, and <q>one variable has no pivot</q>
        may all provide evidence related to the same controlled-vocabulary term.
        The original wording must still remain available for comparison.
      </p>

      <p>
        The extraction just described is difficult to automate and is one of the
        tasks we seek to hand off to a large language model. The tool's current
        deterministic baseline simply looks for a single connector word
        (<q>because</q>, <q>so</q>, <q>therefore</q>, and the like), splits the
        sentence into a premise and a conclusion, and maps each to a
        controlled-vocabulary term, flagging anything it cannot map as out of
        vocabulary. It
        produces at most one premise-to-conclusion step and does not, on its own,
        segment a response into several episodes or classify their relationships.
        Multi-episode segmentation and the chained, independent, and nested 
        distinctions are, for now, human-coder work.
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Stage 3 pseudocode</title>

        <program language="text">
          For each response that continues past screening:

              read the question and the original response

              identify statements that function as:
                  observations or criteria
                  connections or reasoning rules
                  conclusions or judgments

              group the statements into local episodes

              for each proposed episode:
                  record the criterion if supported
                  record the inferential link if supported
                  record the judgment if supported

                  mark any missing component
                  mark any ambiguous interpretation
                  mark any term outside the controlled vocabulary

              determine the likely order and relationship of the episodes

              preserve the original text supporting each proposed component

              record the extracted episode sequence and rationale
        </program>
      </paragraphs>

      <p>
        Extraction is usually one of the most interpretive stages. Two reasonable
        coders may disagree about episode boundaries, whether a link is strongly
        implied, or whether two statements form one episode or a chain of two
        episodes. These disagreements should be recorded rather than hidden.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-stage-match">
      <title>Stage 4: Match</title>

      <p>
        The matching stage compares each extracted episode with the current
        codebook. The purpose is to identify possible correspondences between
        the reasoning in the response and the project's named control structures.
      </p>

      <p>
        Matching is not necessarily a one-code decision. An episode may:
        <ul>
          <li>match one code clearly;</li>
          <li>match several codes;</li>
          <li>partially match a code;</li>
          <li>contain terminology not represented in the codebook; or</li>
          <li>match no existing code.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The matcher should preserve every plausible candidate rather than
        selecting a winner prematurely. A later review can determine whether one
        candidate is preferable, several codes should be retained, or the
        codebook needs revision.
      </p>

      <p>
        Matching must use question-specific information. A general phrase such
        as <q>there is a pivot</q> does not have the same meaning in every
        problem. The prompt determines which criteria, judgments, and links are
        mathematically relevant.
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Stage 4 pseudocode</title>

        <program language="text">
          For each extracted episode:

              load the codebook version assigned to the analysis

              load the coding information for the current question

              for each codebook entry:
                  compare the episode's criterion with the code definition
                  compare the episode's judgment with the code definition
                  compare the inferential link with the question-specific rules
                  check inclusion and exclusion examples

                  if the episode plausibly fits:
                      record the code as a candidate
                      record which evidence produced the match
                      record a short rationale

              if no code fits:
                  record NO MATCH

              if unfamiliar terminology is present:
                  record an out-of-vocabulary flag

              preserve all plausible candidates for disposition and review
        </program>
      </paragraphs>

      <p>
        A match is a proposal, not a final code. The match indicates that an
        episode satisfies enough of a codebook entry to deserve consideration.
      </p>

      <p>
        As with the other stages, the comparison just described is the target.
        The current deterministic matcher is simpler: it looks for codebook
        keyword patterns and surface-marker phrases that appear as substrings of
        a step's premise or conclusion, and it reports every code whose pattern
        or marker fires. A <q>candidate code</q> from the baseline therefore
        means only that a codebook phrase occurred in the step text. Weighing it
        against the full code definition, its inclusion and exclusion rules, and
        the question-specific inferential links is the human reviewer's job.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-stage-dispose">
      <title>Stage 5: Dispose</title>

      <p>
        The disposition stage decides where a response should go next. It uses
        the structured outputs of screening, extraction, and matching. It should
        not introduce a new interpretation of the student's reasoning.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pipeline records the disposition as one of six values. In the
        deterministic baseline they are assigned by a simple cascade over the
        outputs of the earlier stages:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title><c>auto_accept</c></title>
            <p>
              Exactly one codebook code matched a clean chain with no flags. The
              reviewer's role is confirmation only. Automatic acceptance should
              be trusted only after the project has validated the rule on
              human-coded data; during early development, even these may be sent
              to a human.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title><c>standard_review</c></title>
            <p>
              Two or more distinct codes matched. More than one code plausibly
              describes the reasoning, so a human confirms or edits the
              candidates. A response with several plausible codes is routed here;
              there is no separate <q>multiple-match</q> destination.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title><c>terminology_flag</c></title>
            <p>
              A chain step used a term outside the controlled vocabulary (an
              out-of-vocabulary step). This signals a possible vocabulary gap
              rather than a coding error.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title><c>no_match</c></title>
            <p>
              A chain was extracted, but no codebook pattern fired on any step.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title><c>escalate</c></title>
            <p>
              The response needs full manual coding: anything the screener
              marked <c>EXCLUDE</c> or <c>FLAG</c>, or any response on which
              chain extraction failed to produce usable steps.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title><c>problem_type_flag</c></title>
            <p>
              A matched code appears inappropriate for the question type. This
              value is defined in the schema but is <em>not</em> produced by the
              current deterministic disposer, which has no question-type lookup;
              it is reserved for a later, more capable disposer.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Stage 5 pseudocode</title>

        <program language="text">
          For each response:

              read the screening result
              read the extracted chain and its steps
              read all candidate matches and flags

              if the screener said EXCLUDE or FLAG:         assign escalate
              else if no chain was extracted, or the chain
                      has no steps:                         assign escalate
              else if any chain step is out of vocabulary:  assign terminology_flag
              else if no codebook code matched:             assign no_match
              else if two or more distinct codes matched:   assign standard_review
              else (exactly one code matched):              assign auto_accept

              record the disposition and the rule that triggered it
        </program>
      </paragraphs>

      <p>
        Because the current disposer is a rule cascade over shallow baseline
        outputs, even an <c>auto_accept</c> should be trusted only after the
        project has validated the rule on human-coded data. During early
        development, every case (clear or not) may be sent to human
        review so that the research team can evaluate the pipeline. The
        <xref ref="ch-pipeline-demo" text="title"/> shows five responses reaching
        five different dispositions, one per branch of this cascade.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-pipeline-human-review">
      <title>Human Review Across the Pipeline</title>

      <p>
        A reviewer should be able to see the complete analytic packet for a
        response:
        <ul>
          <li>the original question;</li>
          <li>the original response;</li>
          <li>the screening decision and rationale;</li>
          <li>the proposed episodes;</li>
          <li>the evidence used for each criterion, link, and judgment;</li>
          <li>all candidate codes;</li>
          <li>all uncertainty and vocabulary flags; and</li>
          <li>the proposed disposition.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The reviewer may:
        <ul>
          <li>confirm the proposed analysis;</li>
          <li>change the screening decision;</li>
          <li>split, combine, reorder, or remove episodes;</li>
          <li>revise a criterion, inferential link, or judgment;</li>
          <li>add or remove candidate codes;</li>
          <li>record the correctness of a complete episode;</li>
          <li>mark the case as unresolved; or</li>
          <li>recommend a codebook or vocabulary revision.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The tool should preserve both its original proposal and the human
        decision. The difference between them is analytically valuable because it
        helps the research team identify recurring error types and improve the
        framework, codebook, prompts, and review procedures.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-pipeline-audit">
      <title>What the Pipeline Must Preserve</title>

      <p>
        The pipeline is designed to make the analysis reproducible and
        inspectable. For every result, the project should preserve enough
        information to determine:
        <ul>
          <li>which response and question were analyzed;</li>
          <li>which source file supplied the response;</li>
          <li>which codebook and controlled-vocabulary versions were used;</li>
          <li>which tool, model, rules, or prompt version produced the output;</li>
          <li>what each stage proposed;</li>
          <li>which stage produced an uncertainty or failure;</li>
          <li>what the human reviewer decided; and</li>
          <li>when and why a later revision occurred.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This preservation of intermediate decisions is one of the central
        differences between an auditable research pipeline and a system that
        simply assigns a final label.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 5: HUMAN DECISION POINTS                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-human-decisions">
    <title>Human Decision Points</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The pipeline can organize responses, propose episodes, identify possible
        codebook matches, and direct difficult cases to review. It cannot remove
        the need for human interpretation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Human researchers remain responsible for deciding what the response
        provides evidence for, whether the proposed analysis is mathematically
        defensible, and whether the current coding rules are adequate. These
        decisions occur throughout the pipeline rather than only after the tool
        has finished.
      </p>

      <p>
        Human review is especially important when:
        <ul>
          <li>the response is brief, incomplete, or ambiguous;</li>
          <li>part of the reasoning is not explicitly stated;</li>
          <li>the response may contain more than one episode;</li>
          <li>an operation may be confused with a control;</li>
          <li>more than one codebook entry appears to fit;</li>
          <li>the response uses terminology outside the controlled vocabulary;</li>
          <li>the response contains contradictory statements;</li>
          <li>the validity of an inference depends on the specific question;</li>
          <li>
            the response contains sound local reasoning but does not complete the
            requested task; or
          </li>
          <li>the codebook itself may need clarification or revision.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-decision-types">
      <title>Different Kinds of Human Decisions</title>

      <p>
        Human review involves several distinct kinds of decisions. These should
        not be combined into one general judgment such as <q>the response looks
        right</q>.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-human-decisions">
        <title>Major human decision points in the analysis.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="22%"/>
          <col width="40%"/>
          <col width="38%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Decision</cell>
            <cell>Main question</cell>
            <cell>Possible outcome</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Screening</cell>
            <cell>
              Does the response contain enough interpretable justification to
              analyze?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Include, exclude, or route the response for closer review.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Episode segmentation</cell>
            <cell>
              How many local episodes are present, and where are their
              boundaries?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              One episode, several episodes, an incomplete episode, or an
              unresolved segmentation.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Episode interpretation</cell>
            <cell>
              What evidence supports the proposed criterion, inferential link,
              and judgment?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Confirm, revise, remove, or mark a component as missing.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Codebook matching</cell>
            <cell>
              Which control-structure codes plausibly describe the episode?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Confirm one or more codes, reject a proposed code, or record no
              match.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Correctness</cell>
            <cell>
              Is the criterion true, and does it validly support the judgment?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Assign the appropriate correctness annotation or mark the episode
              incomplete.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Goal coverage</cell>
            <cell>
              Does the response answer the full question?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Complete, partially complete, or not addressed.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Framework revision</cell>
            <cell>
              Does the disagreement reveal a problem in the vocabulary,
              codebook, or coding policy?
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Revise guidance, add an example, propose a new term, or preserve an
              unresolved case.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-evidence-before-interpretation">
      <title>Evidence Before Interpretation</title>

      <p>
        A reviewer should begin with the evidence visible in the response before
        deciding how that evidence should be coded. The goal is to represent the
        justification the student provided, not the stronger argument the student
        might have intended.
      </p>

      <p>
        A useful sequence of questions is:
        <ol>
          <li>
            What words, symbols, diagrams, or calculations are actually present?
          </li>
          <li>
            Which part of the response appears to function as a criterion?
          </li>
          <li>What judgment did the student actually state?</li>
          <li>
            Is the connection between the criterion and judgment explicit,
            strongly supported, ambiguous, or missing?
          </li>
          <li>
            Would identifying a component require me to add mathematical
            reasoning not supplied by the student?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        The final question is especially important. A reviewer may understand
        what a student was probably trying to say, but a probable intention is not
        always sufficient evidence for a complete episode.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reviewers should distinguish among:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Paraphrasing</title>
            <p>
              Restating the student's idea in clearer or more standardized
              language without changing its mathematical content.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Interpreting</title>
            <p>
              Identifying the mathematical role of a statement using evidence
              supplied by the response and question.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Repairing</title>
            <p>
              Adding a missing condition, theorem, intermediate result, or
              conclusion so that the reasoning becomes mathematically complete.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Paraphrasing and interpretation are necessary parts of coding. Repairing
        a response is not. If repair would be required, the missing component
        should remain missing.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-screening">
      <title>Human Decisions During Screening</title>

      <p>
        Screening asks whether a response contains enough justification to enter
        the episode-analysis process. It does not ask whether the response is
        mathematically correct.
      </p>

      <p>
        A clearly incorrect explanation may still be codable because it reveals
        a criterion, an inferential link, and a judgment. Conversely, a correct
        final answer may be non-codable if no connection between the work and the
        conclusion is provided.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reviewers may need to decide how to handle responses such as:
        <ul>
          <li>a correct calculation with no written conclusion;</li>
          <li>a conclusion accompanied by unexplained symbolic work;</li>
          <li>a statement that is relevant but too vague to identify a criterion;</li>
          <li>
            a response that uses informal language but expresses a clear
            mathematical relationship;
          </li>
          <li>a response that appears to answer a different question; or</li>
          <li>
            a response that contains both codable reasoning and unrelated text.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        When the current screening rule does not clearly determine the outcome,
        the reviewer should:
        <ol>
          <li>preserve the response;</li>
          <li>record the uncertainty;</li>
          <li>apply the current conservative review policy; and</li>
          <li>
            note whether the case suggests that the screening guidance needs a
            new example or clarification.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-segmentation">
      <title>Deciding How to Divide a Response into Episodes</title>

      <p>
        Episode segmentation is the process of deciding how many local
        justifications appear in a response and where one episode ends and
        another begins.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There are fewer pivots than variables, so there is a free variable.
          Therefore, the solution is not unique.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        A reviewer might reasonably identify two episodes:
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
            e_1 \amp: \text{fewer pivots than variables}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{a free variable exists},\\
            e_2 \amp: \text{a free variable exists}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{the solution is not unique}.
          \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The same response also raises a nesting question: should the larger
        reasoning chain receive one code, should both local episodes receive
        codes, or should only the most specific episode be coded?
      </p>

      <p>
        The research team must adopt a consistent
        <term>nesting policy</term>. The policy may direct reviewers to:
        <ul>
          <li>code both the parent and child episodes;</li>
          <li>code only the larger parent episode; or</li>
          <li>code only the more specific child episode.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This choice is a coding convention. It is not determined automatically by
        the mathematics. Reviewers should therefore apply the approved policy
        rather than making a new decision for each response.
      </p>

      <p>
        When episode boundaries are uncertain, reviewers should record the
        competing segmentations. A disagreement about the number of episodes is
        different from a disagreement about which code applies, and the two
        should be analyzed separately.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-operation-control">
      <title>Distinguishing Operations from Controls</title>

      <p>
        Reviewers must sometimes decide whether a statement describes an
        operation, a control, or both.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Operation</title>
            <p><q>I row reduced the matrix.</q></p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Control</title>
            <p>
              <q>The reduced matrix has no contradictory row, so the system is
              consistent.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The operation produces a transformed matrix. The control evaluates a
        feature of that matrix and supports a judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some statements do not fit neatly into only one category. A theorem,
        intermediate result, or calculation may function as an operation in one
        part of the response and as a criterion in another. Reviewers should ask:
        <ul>
          <li>Is the statement producing a new mathematical object?</li>
          <li>Is it evaluating or interpreting an existing result?</li>
          <li>Is it being used to justify a decision?</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Ambiguous cases should be documented. The purpose of the distinction is
        to support consistent analysis, not to force every mathematical
        statement into an artificial category.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-matching">
      <title>Deciding Which Codebook Entries Apply</title>

      <p>
        The matching stage may propose one or more candidate codes. A human
        reviewer decides whether those candidates accurately describe the
        episode.
      </p>

      <p>
        A reviewer should compare the episode with:
        <ul>
          <li>the code definition;</li>
          <li>the inclusion rules;</li>
          <li>the exclusion rules;</li>
          <li>positive examples;</li>
          <li>near-miss examples;</li>
          <li>confusable codes; and</li>
          <li>the question-specific criterion-to-judgment relationships.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The reviewer should not select a code merely because the response
        contains a familiar keyword. For example, the word <q>pivot</q> may
        appear while the actual justification depends on dimension, consistency,
        or a comparison between the number of variables and pivots.
      </p>

      <p>
        A response may legitimately receive more than one code when it contains
        multiple independent control structures. However, a single idea should
        not automatically receive several codes simply because multiple codebook
        descriptions use similar terminology.
      </p>

      <p>
        When several codes appear plausible, the reviewer should determine
        whether:
        <ul>
          <li>the response contains several distinct episodes;</li>
          <li>the same episode genuinely enacts more than one control;</li>
          <li>one code is more specific than another;</li>
          <li>the code definitions overlap; or</li>
          <li>the response is too ambiguous to resolve confidently.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-correctness">
      <title>Evaluating Correctness</title>

      <p>
        Correctness is evaluated only after an episode has been identified. The
        project separates two questions:
        <ol>
          <li>Is the criterion true in the mathematical situation?</li>
          <li>
            Does the criterion validly support the judgment for this question?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        These questions are independent. A student may cite a true fact but use
        it to support an invalid conclusion. A student may also state a valid
        general relationship but apply it to a criterion that is false in the
        given problem.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a complete episode
        <md>
          e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k),
        </md>
        the project may record a two-part correctness annotation
        <md>
          \operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c_k^1,c_k^2),
        </md>
        where:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>c_k^1</m> records whether the criterion is true; and
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>c_k^2</m> records whether the inferential link validly supports
            the judgment.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        An incomplete episode should not be forced into this scheme. If the link
        or judgment cannot be identified, the reviewer should preserve the
        episode as incomplete and route it according to the approved review
        policy.
      </p>

      <p>
        Correctness must be judged against the exact question and an agreed
        <term>normative reference</term>. Reviewers should not rely only on a
        general impression that the response <q>sounds mathematically right.</q>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-goal-coverage">
      <title>Separating Justification Quality from Goal Coverage</title>

      <p>
        A response may contain a sound episode without answering the complete
        question. The project therefore distinguishes
        <term>justification quality</term> from <term>goal coverage</term>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider a question that asks a student to:
        <ol>
          <li>determine whether a solution is unique; and</li>
          <li>identify the basic and free variables.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        The response
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There are two pivots, so one variable is free.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        may contain a valid local episode, but it does not state whether the
        solution is unique and does not identify the free variable. The local
        justification may be sound even though the answer is incomplete.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reviewers should therefore ask two separate questions:
        <ul>
          <li>Is the reasoning within each episode mathematically valid?</li>
          <li>Do the episodes collectively address every part of the prompt?</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A correct final answer does not guarantee valid justification, and valid
        local justification does not guarantee complete goal coverage.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-oov">
      <title>Out-of-Vocabulary and No-Match Cases</title>

      <p>
        An <term>out-of-vocabulary</term> case occurs when a response expresses a
        mathematically relevant idea that is not represented in the current
        controlled vocabulary. A <term>no-match</term> case occurs when an
        episode can be extracted but no current codebook entry describes it
        adequately.
      </p>

      <p>
        These cases should not be forced into the nearest available category.
        A reviewer should determine whether the case reflects:
        <ul>
          <li>an unfamiliar way of expressing an existing idea;</li>
          <li>a missing synonym in the controlled vocabulary;</li>
          <li>a genuinely new criterion or control structure;</li>
          <li>an episode that is too incomplete to classify;</li>
          <li>a codebook definition that is too narrow; or</li>
          <li>reasoning that is relevant but outside the current analytic scope.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A new term or code should not be added on the basis of one unusual
        response without review. The research team should examine similar cases,
        compare them with existing codes, and document the reason for any
        extension.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-overrides">
      <title>Confirmations, Overrides, and Unresolved Cases</title>

      <p>
        After reviewing a proposed analysis, a human researcher may:
        <ul>
          <li>confirm it without change;</li>
          <li>revise one or more episode components;</li>
          <li>split or combine episodes;</li>
          <li>add or remove candidate codes;</li>
          <li>change the screening decision;</li>
          <li>change the workflow disposition;</li>
          <li>mark the analysis as unresolved; or</li>
          <li>recommend a revision to the coding guidance.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The tool's original proposal should not be overwritten. The project
        preserves both the proposal and the human decision so that disagreements
        can be studied later. On the candidate codes specifically, each reviewer
        edit is recorded as one of a fixed set of actions:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <c>confirm</c>, 
          <c>add</c>, 
          <c>remove</c>, 
          <c>replace</c>, or 
          <c>reject_all</c>
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        and appended to an <term>override log</term> rather than replacing what the
        tool proposed. The log is append-only precisely so that the difference
        between the tool's proposal and the human decision remains available for
        study.
      </p>

      <p>
        An <term>override</term> is not automatically evidence that the tool
        failed. The override may instead reveal:
        <ul>
          <li>a tool error;</li>
          <li>an ambiguous response;</li>
          <li>a reasonable alternative interpretation;</li>
          <li>an unclear code definition;</li>
          <li>an inconsistent human decision;</li>
          <li>a missing vocabulary term; or</li>
          <li>a question-specific rule that was not represented correctly.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Reviewers should provide a brief rationale whenever they override a
        proposed analysis. A change without a rationale is difficult to audit and
        provides little information for improving the system.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some cases may remain unresolved after discussion. It is better to
        preserve a genuine ambiguity than to create artificial agreement.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-adjudication">
      <title>Disagreement and Adjudication</title>

      <p>
        When two coders disagree, the first task is to identify the level at
        which the disagreement occurred.
      </p>

      <p>
        Common disagreement types include:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening disagreement</title>
            <p>
              The coders disagree about whether the response contains enough
              justification to analyze.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Segmentation disagreement</title>
            <p>
              The coders identify different numbers or boundaries of episodes.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Component disagreement</title>
            <p>
              The coders identify different criteria, inferential links, or
              judgments.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Code disagreement</title>
            <p>
              The coders agree on the episode but assign different control-
              structure codes.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness disagreement</title>
            <p>
              The coders disagree about whether the criterion is true or the
              inference is valid.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Policy disagreement</title>
            <p>
              The coders are applying different nesting, screening, or
              multiple-code conventions.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        During adjudication, reviewers should:
        <ol>
          <li>return to the original response and question;</li>
          <li>identify the exact point of disagreement;</li>
          <li>consult the current codebook and coding policy;</li>
          <li>state the evidence supporting each interpretation;</li>
          <li>reach a decision when the evidence and rules support one;</li>
          <li>
            preserve the case as unresolved when the evidence does not support a
            defensible decision; and
          </li>
          <li>
            update the coding guidance when the disagreement exposes a recurring
            weakness.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Adjudication should improve the shared analytic method, not merely force
        coders to agree on a spreadsheet entry.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-rationales">
      <title>Writing a Useful Review Rationale</title>

      <p>
        A good rationale is brief, specific, and tied to visible evidence. It
        should explain why the reviewer made the decision without speculating
        about the student's internal thoughts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Useful rationales include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <q>Included because the response states a criterion and connects it
            to a conclusion using the phrase <q>so</q>.</q>
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>The judgment is missing; the response states only that a free
            variable exists.</q>
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>Two episodes are present because the first judgment becomes the
            criterion of the second.</q>
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>Removed the pivot code because pivots are mentioned only as part
            of an operation; the stated justification relies on dimension.</q>
          </li>
          <li>
            <q>Marked OOV because the response uses a relevant criterion not
            represented in the current vocabulary.</q>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Avoid rationales such as:
        <ul>
          <li><q>The student understands the concept.</q></li>
          <li><q>This is obviously what the student meant.</q></li>
          <li><q>The response seems wrong.</q></li>
          <li><q>I would have solved it differently.</q></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        These statements are too broad, unsupported, or unrelated to the coding
        decision.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-human-checklist">
      <title>Reviewer Checklist</title>

      <p>
        Before finalizing a review, ask:
        <ol>
          <li>Did I read the original question and the complete response?</li>
          <li>
            Did I separate what is explicitly stated from what I inferred?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I avoid repairing missing reasoning?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I apply the current episode-boundary and nesting policies?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I distinguish operations from enacted controls?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I consider every plausible code rather than relying on keywords?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I evaluate criterion correctness and inferential validity
            separately?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I evaluate goal coverage separately from local justification?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I preserve uncertainty, OOV cases, and unresolved disagreements?
          </li>
          <li>
            Did I record a rationale that refers to evidence in the response?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 6: EXAMPLES                                          -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-examples">
    <title>Worked Examples</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This section applies the framework to several short responses. The
        examples are ordered from relatively clear cases to cases requiring more
        human interpretation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each example considers some or all of the following questions:
        <ol>
          <li>Should the response continue past screening?</li>
          <li>How many episodes appear in the response?</li>
          <li>
            What are the criterion, inferential link, and judgment in each
            episode?
          </li>
          <li>How are multiple episodes related?</li>
          <li>Which control-structure codes might apply?</li>
          <li>
            Is the criterion true, and is the inferential link mathematically
            valid?
          </li>
          <li>Does the response address the complete goal of the question?</li>
          <li>What human decision, if any, remains unresolved?</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Some response texts below come from existing project documents or
        published studies. Others are constructed examples designed to isolate a
        particular coding issue. Constructed examples are identified explicitly.
        Student identifiers are omitted because they are not needed for the
        analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
        The analyses are intended to demonstrate how the framework is used. A
        final research code should always be based on the current question,
        codebook, controlled vocabulary, nesting policy, and human-review
        protocol.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-context">
      <title>A Common Question Context</title>

      <p>
        Several examples use a question that presents an augmented matrix in
        reduced row echelon form, states that the corresponding system is
        consistent, and asks the student to:
        <ul>
          <li>determine whether the solution is unique; and</li>
          <li>identify the basic and free variables.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose the matrix has three variable columns and two pivot columns. The
        agreed mathematical reference is:
        <ul>
          <li>two variables are basic;</li>
          <li>one variable is free;</li>
          <li>the solution is not unique; and</li>
          <li>
            because the system is known to be consistent, it has infinitely many
            solutions.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This context is important. The same statement may support different
        conclusions in a different problem.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 1: COMPLETE SINGLE EPISODE                            -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-complete">
      <title>Example 1: One Complete Episode</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        There is a free variable, so the solution is not unique.
      </program>

      <p>
        This is the kind of clean, single-episode justification that passes through 
        all stages of the pipeline with an <c>auto_accept</c> label. Here is the 
        complete process:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>
            <p>
              The response contains a mathematical criterion and connects it to a
              judgment. It should therefore be included for episode analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>
            <p>
              The response contains one complete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>
                  <p>A free variable exists.</p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>
                  <p>
                    A system with a free variable cannot have exactly one solution.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>
                  <p>The solution is not unique.</p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The episode is a candidate for a control structure based on the
              existence of a free variable. In the stated question context:
              <ul>
                <li>the criterion is true; and</li>
                <li>the inferential link is valid.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The local episode would receive
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              This is a relatively clear case. The main human task is to confirm that
              the codebook entry matches the complete criterion-to-judgment
              relationship rather than matching only the phrase
              <q>free variable.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 2: INCOMPLETE EPISODE                                -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-incomplete">
      <title>Example 2: An Incomplete Episode</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        We cannot determine the value of the free variable.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response is mathematically relevant and provides evidence about
              how the student interprets a free variable. It is not blank,
              unrelated, or merely a bare answer. It should therefore be preserved
              for episode analysis and human review.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response suggests an incomplete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    A free variable is present.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing or insufficiently expressed.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              The phrase <q>we cannot determine the value</q> describes something
              the student associates with a free variable, but it does not clearly
              state a judgment about uniqueness, the number of solutions, or the
              complete solution set.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The response may be related to a control structure involving free
              variables. However, the missing inferential link and judgment prevent
              the episode from being matched confidently to a complete
              criterion-to-judgment code.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The episode should not receive a two-part correctness code because
              the inferential link and judgment cannot be identified.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              A reviewer may expect the student to continue with
              <q>therefore, the solution is not unique</q> or
              <q>therefore, there are infinitely many solutions.</q> The response
              does not state either conclusion. The reviewer must preserve the
              episode as incomplete rather than selecting a conclusion for the
              student.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 3: CHAINED EPISODES                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-chain">
      <title>Example 3: A Chain of Two Episodes</title>

      <p>
        Consider the following constructed response:
      </p>

      <program>
        There are fewer pivots than variables, so one variable is free.
        Therefore, the solution is not unique.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains mathematical criteria, inferential
              connections, and judgments. It should therefore be included for
              episode analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response contains two complete episodes:
              <md>
                E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2\rangle,
              </md>
              where
              <md>
                \begin{aligned}
                  e_1
                  &amp;=
                  (\text{fewer pivots than variables},
                  \text{an unpivoted variable is free},
                  \text{a free variable exists}),\\
                  e_2
                  &amp;=
                  (\text{a free variable exists},
                  \text{a free variable rules out exactly one solution},
                  \text{the solution is not unique}).
                \end{aligned}
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              The episodes are chained because the judgment of <m>e_1</m> becomes
              the criterion of <m>e_2</m>:
              <md>
                J_1=C_2.
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The first episode is a candidate for a control structure based on
              comparing the number of pivots with the number of variables. The
              second is a candidate for a control structure based on the existence
              of a free variable.
            </p>

            <p>
              The response should not be assigned only one code merely because both
              episodes involve pivots and variables. The two criterion-to-judgment
              relationships are distinct.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              In the stated question context, both criteria are true and both
              inferential links are valid. Thus,
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1)
                \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                \operatorname{code}(e_2)=(1,1).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must apply the project's nesting policy consistently.
              The two mathematical steps are visible, but the policy determines
              whether both episodes receive separate codes or whether one is
              treated as part of a larger episode.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 4: MULTIPLE CONTROL STRUCTURES                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-multiple-controls">
      <title>Example 4: Several Control Structures in One Response</title>

      <p>
        In a published exam analysis, students were asked whether the columns of a
        matrix span <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        2 pivots implies dim(C(A)) = 2, and dim(C(A)) is less than
        dim(R^3), so the columns of A cannot span R^3 by the
        Goldilocks Theorem.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains several connected mathematical claims and a
              final conclusion. It should therefore be included for episode
              analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              One possible segmentation contains three chained episodes:
              <md>
                E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,e_3\rangle,
              </md>
              where
              <md>
                \begin{aligned}
                  e_1
                  &amp;=
                  (\text{the matrix has two pivots},
                  \text{the number of pivots determines the column-space dimension},
                  \dim(C(A))=2),\\
                  e_2
                  &amp;=
                  (\dim(C(A))=2&lt;3,
                  \text{a two-dimensional subspace cannot equal }\mathbb{R}^3,
                  C(A)\ne\mathbb{R}^3),\\
                  e_3
                  &amp;=
                  (C(A)\ne\mathbb{R}^3,
                  \text{the columns span exactly the column space},
                  \text{the columns do not span }\mathbb{R}^3).
                \end{aligned}
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              Another defensible segmentation could combine the final two steps
              into one larger episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The response contains evidence for control structures involving:
              <ul>
                <li>pivots;</li>
                <li>dimension or column space; and</li>
                <li>the Goldilocks Theorem.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              These matches arise from the roles the ideas play in the argument,
              not merely from the presence of the words <q>pivots</q>,
              <q>dimension</q>, and <q>theorem.</q>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              Assuming the matrix has exactly two pivots, the criteria and
              inferential links in the proposed chain are mathematically valid.
              Each complete episode would therefore receive
              <m>(1,1)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              Reviewers may agree on the mathematical argument but disagree about:
              <ul>
                <li>the number of episodes;</li>
                <li>whether some episodes are nested;</li>
                <li>which controls should receive separate codes; or</li>
                <li>
                  whether a theorem-based code includes the intermediate
                  dimension-based reasoning.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              These are segmentation and coding-policy decisions rather than
              disagreements about whether the response contains mathematical
              justification.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 5: OOV                                               -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-oov">
      <title>Example 5: Relevant but Out-of-Vocabulary Reasoning</title>

      <p>
        For a question involving an augmented matrix, consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        It depends what is in the augmented matrix.
      </program>

      <p>
        This is one of the responses traced through the whole pipeline in the
        <xref ref="ch-pipeline-demo" text="title"/>, where its out-of-vocabulary
        phrasing produces a <c>terminology_flag</c> disposition.
      </p>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response is not blank or unrelated. It expresses a conditional
              claim about the information needed to answer the question. A
              conservative screening decision should preserve it for human review.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              One possible episode is:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The contents of the augmented matrix may vary.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The requested classification depends on information in the
                    augmented column.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The answer cannot be determined from the stated information.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              The judgment is not stated directly. A reviewer may instead decide
              that the response contains an incomplete or ambiguous episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              Suppose the controlled vocabulary includes pivots, free variables,
              consistency, and numbers of solutions, but does not include a term
              for reasoning that the answer depends on the augmented column. The
              response should then receive an OOV flag.
            </p>

            <p>
              It should not be forced into a pivot or consistency code merely
              because those ideas may be relevant to the question.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              A correctness code should be assigned only if the reviewer determines
              that the episode is complete and the exact question provides enough
              information to evaluate the criterion and link.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must decide whether the response represents:
              <ul>
                <li>unfamiliar wording for an existing control;</li>
                <li>a new candidate vocabulary term;</li>
                <li>a new control structure;</li>
                <li>an incomplete episode; or</li>
                <li>reasoning outside the current analytic scope.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 6: NO MATCH                                          -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-no-match">
      <title>Example 6: Extractable Reasoning with No Codebook Match</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        It is the same because, as we discussed in class, only four
        pivot points would be available.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains a mathematical feature and an attempted
              justification. It should therefore be included for analysis, although
              the referent of <q>the same</q> may require the original question to
              interpret.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              One possible episode is:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Only four pivot positions are available.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The limitation on pivot positions leaves the relevant property
                    unchanged.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The relevant mathematical result is the same.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              Both the meaning of <q>the same</q> and the precise inferential link
              remain unclear without the original question.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The response may use vocabulary already represented in the project,
              such as pivots, while still failing to match an existing codebook
              rule. This would be a <term>no-match</term> case rather than
              necessarily an OOV case.
            </p>

            <p>
              In an OOV case, the mathematical idea itself is missing from the
              controlled vocabulary. In a no-match case, the vocabulary may
              represent the idea, but the codebook contains no adequate
              criterion-to-judgment classification.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The episode should not receive a correctness code until the original
              question clarifies the judgment and the mathematical relationship
              being asserted.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer should determine whether the difficulty results from:
              <ul>
                <li>missing question context;</li>
                <li>an incomplete inferential link;</li>
                <li>an inadequate codebook;</li>
                <li>an incorrect extraction; or</li>
                <li>a genuinely new control structure.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 7: CORRECT CRITERION, INVALID LINK                    -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-invalid-link">
      <title>Example 7: A True Criterion with an Invalid Link</title>

      <p>
        Consider the following constructed response:
      </p>

      <program>
        There is a free variable, so the system is inconsistent.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains a criterion, an inferential link, and a
              judgment. It should therefore be included for episode analysis even
              though the conclusion is mathematically incorrect.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response contains one complete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    A free variable exists.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    A system with a free variable is inconsistent.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The system is inconsistent.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The episode is a candidate for a control structure involving free
              variables and consistency. The match should preserve the student's
              actual criterion-to-judgment relationship rather than replacing it
              with the correct relationship between free variables and
              non-uniqueness.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              In the stated question context:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  the criterion is true because a free variable exists; but
                </li>
                <li>
                  the inferential link is invalid because a consistent system may
                  have a free variable and infinitely many solutions.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              Therefore,
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,0).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer should preserve the distinction between identifying a
              true mathematical feature and using that feature incorrectly.
              Labeling the entire response only as <q>incorrect</q> would hide this
              distinction.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 8: GOAL COVERAGE                                     -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-goal-coverage">
      <title>Example 8: Sound Local Reasoning but Incomplete Goal Coverage</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        We only have two pivot points, so one variable will be free.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains a criterion, an inferential link, and a
              judgment. It should therefore be included for episode analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response contains one complete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    There are two pivots among three variables.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Fewer pivots than variables leaves at least one variable
                    unconstrained.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    One variable is free.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The episode is a candidate for a control structure based on comparing
              the number of pivots with the number of variables.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              In the given matrix, the criterion is true and the inferential link is
              valid. Therefore,
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Goal coverage</title>

            <p>
              The response does not complete all parts of the prompt:
              <ul>
                <li>it does not state whether the solution is unique;</li>
                <li>it does not identify which variable is free;</li>
                <li>it does not identify the basic variables; and</li>
                <li>
                  it does not explicitly state the number of solutions.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              The response therefore contains sound local justification but only
              partial goal coverage.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must not lower the episode's correctness code merely
              because the response is incomplete. Episode correctness and goal
              coverage are separate annotations.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EXAMPLE 9: CORRECT ANSWER, NO JUSTIFICATION                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-bare-answer">
      <title>Example 9: A Correct Answer without Codable Justification</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        No, the solution is not unique.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response states a judgment but does not provide a mathematical
              criterion or an inferential link. Under a screening policy requiring
              identifiable justification, it should be excluded from episode
              analysis or routed according to the project's bare-answer policy.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response provides:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The solution is not unique.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              Because the criterion and inferential link are absent, the response
              does not contain a complete codable episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The phrase <q>not unique</q> may resemble the judgment used in a
              free-variable control structure, but there is no evidence that the
              student actually used a free variable, a pivot count, or any other
              particular criterion.
            </p>

            <p>
              The response should therefore not be assigned a control-structure
              code based only on the final answer.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The final answer is mathematically correct in the stated question
              context. However, no episode-level correctness code can be assigned
              because the response does not provide a complete episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must apply the screening policy consistently and avoid
              inferring that the student used the most obvious correct
              justification. Final-answer correctness and codable justification are
              separate properties.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- SUMMARY                                                      -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-examples-summary">
      <title>Summary of the Examples</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-examples-summary">
        <title>The principal analytic issue illustrated by each example.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="12%"/>
          <col width="34%"/>
          <col width="54%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Example</cell>
            <cell>Main issue</cell>
            <cell>Central lesson</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>Complete single episode</cell>
            <cell>
              A criterion, link, and judgment can appear clearly in one sentence.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>Incomplete episode</cell>
            <cell>
              Do not supply a missing link or judgment for the student.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>Chained and nested reasoning</cell>
            <cell>
              Episode existence and episode-coding policy are separate
              questions.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>Several control structures</cell>
            <cell>
              Multiple mathematical ideas require analysis of their roles, not
              keyword counting.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>Out-of-vocabulary reasoning</cell>
            <cell>
              Relevant unfamiliar reasoning should be preserved rather than
              forced into an existing term.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>No codebook match</cell>
            <cell>
              Extractable reasoning may still fall outside current codebook
              rules.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>7</cell>
            <cell>True criterion, invalid link</cell>
            <cell>
              Criterion correctness and inferential validity must be evaluated
              separately.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>Partial goal coverage</cell>
            <cell>
              Sound local reasoning does not guarantee a complete answer.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>9</cell>
            <cell>Correct but non-codable answer</cell>
            <cell>
              Final-answer correctness does not provide evidence for a control
              structure.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 7: PRACTICE EXERCISES                                -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-practice">
    <title>Practice Exercises</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The exercises in this section prepare student researchers to analyze
        actual project data. They begin with small recognition tasks and build
        toward complete response coding and calibration.
      </p>

      <p>
        The exercises use synthetic responses written to resemble the kinds of
        short answers found in the project data. They are not responses from the
        research corpus.
      </p>

      <p>
        These exercises restate, in a self-contained form, the rules the
        project's separate <term>Coder's Handbook</term> lays out as the working
        manual. When you begin real coding, that handbook and its coding-sheet
        template are the authority for the exact columns you fill and the values 
        they take.
      </p>

      <p>
        The exercises have four purposes:
        <ol>
          <li>
            practice applying the terminology introduced in this guide;
          </li>
          <li>
            identify parts of the coding protocol that remain unclear;
          </li>
          <li>
            help student researchers become consistent with one another; and
          </li>
          <li>
            produce a record of disagreements that can improve the codebook and
            training materials.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Uncertainty should be recorded rather than hidden. In research coding, an
        accurately described ambiguity is more useful than an unsupported
        confident answer.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-procedure">
      <title>How to Complete the Exercises</title>

      <p>
        For the introductory exercises, students may discuss their reasoning
        while working. For the calibration exercises near the end of the section,
        each student must work independently before comparing results.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unless an exercise gives different directions, use the following
        procedure:
        <ol>
          <li>Read the complete question.</li>
          <li>Read the student response exactly as written.</li>
          <li>
            Record what is explicit, what is strongly supported, and what is
            missing or ambiguous.
          </li>
          <li>
            Apply the current coding rules without silently repairing the
            response.
          </li>
          <li>Give a short rationale tied to visible evidence.</li>
          <li>
            Mark any decision for which the current guide or codebook does not
            provide enough direction.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not consult another student's coding during an independent exercise.
        Independent first-pass coding is necessary if the group later wants to
        measure agreement or identify genuine differences in interpretation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-context">
      <title>Common Mathematical Context</title>

      <p>
        Most of the exercises in this section use the following linear algebra
        question.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-student-guide-practice-question">
        <title>Practice Question A</title>

        <p>
          The reduced row echelon form of the augmented matrix of a system in the
          variables <m>x_1,x_2,x_3</m> is
          <md>
            \left[
            \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2  &amp; 3\\
              0 &amp; 1 &amp; -1 &amp; 4\\
              0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0  &amp; 0
            \end{array}
            \right].
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          Is the system consistent? Does it have a unique solution? Identify the
          basic and free variables. Explain your reasoning.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        The agreed mathematical reference for this question is:
        <ul>
          <li>the system is consistent;</li>
          <li><m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are basic variables;</li>
          <li><m>x_3</m> is a free variable; and</li>
          <li>the system has infinitely many solutions and is not unique.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The reference is supplied because these exercises concern coding, not
        testing the researcher's linear algebra knowledge.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART I: RECOGNIZING EPISODE COMPONENTS                       -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-identify">
      <title>Part I: Identify the Parts of an Episode</title>

      <p>
        For each response below:
        <ol>
          <li>identify the criterion <m>C</m>;</li>
          <li>identify the inferential link <m>I</m>;</li>
          <li>identify the judgment <m>J</m>; and</li>
          <li>
            classify each component as explicit, strongly supported, or missing.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A1.</term>
            <q>
              There is no contradictory row, so the system is consistent.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            One possible analysis is:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <term>Criterion:</term> there is no contradictory row;
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Inferential link:</term> an augmented matrix with no
                contradictory row represents a consistent system; and
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Judgment:</term> the system is consistent.
              </li>
            </ul>
            All three components are explicit or strongly supported.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A2.</term>
            <q>
              Since the third column does not contain a pivot, <m>x_3</m> is
              free.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            One possible analysis is:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <term>Criterion:</term> column three contains no pivot;
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Inferential link:</term> a variable corresponding to a
                non-pivot column is free; and
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Judgment:</term> <m>x_3</m> is free.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A3.</term>
            <q>
              There is a free variable, so the solution is not unique.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            One possible analysis is:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <term>Criterion:</term> a free variable exists;
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Inferential link:</term> a system with a free variable
                cannot have exactly one solution; and
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Judgment:</term> the solution is not unique.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-4">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A4.</term>
            <q>
              There are two pivots.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            The response states a mathematical feature, so it provides a possible
            criterion. It does not state what judgment follows from that
            criterion. The inferential link and judgment are therefore missing.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-5">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A5.</term>
            <q>
              <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are basic variables.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            The response states a judgment, but it does not explain the criterion
            or inferential link used to reach it. A coder should not automatically
            add <q>because columns one and two contain pivots</q>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART II: OPERATIONS AND CONTROLS                              -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-operation-control">
      <title>Part II: Distinguish Operations from Controls</title>

      <p>
        For each statement, classify it as:
        <ul>
          <li>an operation;</li>
          <li>a control;</li>
          <li>both an operation and a control;</li>
          <li>insufficient to determine; or</li>
          <li>neither.</li>
        </ul>
        Explain the classification in one or two sentences.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-operation-control-1">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li><q>I row reduced the matrix.</q></li>
            <li>
              <q>
                The last row is all zeroes, so it does not create a
                contradiction.
              </q>
            </li>
            <li>
              <q>
                Columns one and two contain pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and
                <m>x_2</m> are basic.
              </q>
            </li>
            <li><q>I got the matrix above.</q></li>
            <li>
              <q>
                There is a free variable, so there are infinitely many
                solutions.
              </q>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-operation-control-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why the statement
            <q>I found two pivots</q>
            may require additional context before it can be classified.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART III: SCREENING                                           -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-screen">
      <title>Part III: Screen Responses</title>

      <p>
        For each response:
        <ol>
          <li>
            propose <c>INCLUDE</c>, <c>EXCLUDE</c>, or human review according to
            the current project policy;
          </li>
          <li>give a one-sentence rationale; and</li>
          <li>
            state whether your decision depends on a policy that the group still
            needs to settle.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-screening-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S1.</term>
                <q>No.</q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S2.</term>
                <q>
                  There is a free variable, so the solution cannot be unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S3.</term>
                <md>
                  x_1=3-2x_3,\qquad x_2=4+x_3.
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S4.</term>
                <q>
                  The matrix has two pivots and three variables. Therefore,
                  <m>x_3</m> is free and the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S5.</term>
                <q>
                  I do not understand what the question is asking.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S6.</term>
                <q>
                  It depends.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S7.</term>
                <q>
                  The answer is not unique because the third variable can take
                  any value.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S8.</term>
                <em>No response was submitted.</em>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-screening-policy">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare Responses S3 and S7. Both contain mathematical information,
            but only one explicitly connects that information to a conclusion.
            Explain how the current screening policy should distinguish them.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART IV: SEGMENTATION                                         -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-segment">
      <title>Part IV: Segment Responses into Episodes</title>

      <p>
        For each response:
        <ol>
          <li>determine how many episodes are present;</li>
          <li>write each episode as <m>(C,I,J)</m>;</li>
          <li>
            classify the relationship among the episodes as chained,
            independent, nested, or unresolved; and
          </li>
          <li>
            identify any decision that depends on the project's nesting policy.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E1.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              There are only two pivots for three variables, so one variable is
              free. Since a free variable can take different values, the solution
              is not unique.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E2.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              There is no contradictory row, so the system is consistent. Also,
              there is a free variable, so it has infinitely many solutions.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E3.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              Columns one and two have pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are
              basic. Column three does not have a pivot, so <m>x_3</m> is free.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-4">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E4.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              The system is consistent and <m>x_3</m> is free, which means the
              solution set is a line and therefore there are infinitely many
              solutions.
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            Give at least two plausible segmentations. State which one you prefer
            and what additional coding rule would make the choice more
            consistent.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART V: PRACTICE CODEBOOK                                     -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-codebook">
      <title>Part V: Match Episodes to a Practice Codebook</title>

      <p>
        The following codes are simplified practice codes. They are not the
        official project code IDs and should not be used when coding research
        data.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-practice-codebook">
        <title>Miniature codebook for Practice Question A.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="15%"/>
          <col width="23%"/>
          <col width="36%"/>
          <col width="26%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Code</cell>
            <cell>Name</cell>
            <cell>Include when</cell>
            <cell>Do not include when</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC1</c></cell>
            <cell>Contradictory-row consistency</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses the presence or absence of a contradictory row to
              classify the system as consistent or inconsistent.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              The response merely performs row reduction without interpreting a
              row.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC2</c></cell>
            <cell>Pivot-variable classification</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses pivot or non-pivot columns to classify variables
              as basic or free.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Pivots are mentioned but no variable classification is made.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC3</c></cell>
            <cell>Free-variable uniqueness</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses the existence or absence of free variables to
              decide whether the solution is unique.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              A free variable is identified but no conclusion about uniqueness
              or number of solutions is drawn.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC4</c></cell>
            <cell>Pivot-count comparison</cell>
            <cell>
              The response compares the number of pivots with the number of
              variables to infer that a free variable exists or that every
              variable is basic.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              The response only states the number of pivots without making the
              comparison or drawing the relevant judgment.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><c>PC5</c></cell>
            <cell>Geometric solution-set interpretation</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses the dimension or geometric shape of the solution
              set to justify uniqueness or multiplicity.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Geometric language is used descriptively but does not support a
              judgment.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        For each response below:
        <ol>
          <li>extract the episode or episodes;</li>
          <li>record every plausible practice code;</li>
          <li>identify the textual evidence supporting each match;</li>
          <li>identify any plausible near match that should be rejected; and</li>
          <li>give a short matching rationale.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-practice-match-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M1.</term>
                <q>
                  Column three has no pivot, so <m>x_3</m> is free.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M2.</term>
                <q>
                  There are two pivots but three variables, so one variable must
                  be free.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M3.</term>
                <q>
                  One variable is free, so the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M4.</term>
                <q>
                  There is no row that says zero equals a nonzero number, so the
                  system is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M5.</term>
                <q>
                  The solutions form a line because there is one parameter.
                  Therefore, there are infinitely many solutions.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M6.</term>
                <q>
                  I found two pivots.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-practice-keyword-warning">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why the word <q>pivot</q> is not sufficient by itself to
            assign either <c>PC2</c> or <c>PC4</c>. Use Responses M1, M2, and M6
            in your explanation.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART VI: CORRECTNESS                                          -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-correctness">
      <title>Part VI: Evaluate Criterion and Link Correctness</title>

      <p>
        For each complete episode:
        <ol>
          <li>
            determine whether the criterion is true in Practice Question A;
          </li>
          <li>
            determine whether the inferential link validly supports the judgment;
          </li>
          <li>
            assign the pair <m>(c^1,c^2)</m>; and
          </li>
          <li>
            explain the two digits separately.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Use:
        <ul>
          <li><m>c^1=1</m> when the criterion is true;</li>
          <li><m>c^1=0</m> when the criterion is false;</li>
          <li><m>c^2=1</m> when the inferential link is valid; and</li>
          <li><m>c^2=0</m> when the inferential link is invalid.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-correctness-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C1.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_3</m> is free, so the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C2.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_3</m> is free, so the solution is unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C3.</term>
                <q>
                  There are three pivots, so every variable is basic and the
                  solution is unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C4.</term>
                <q>
                  The matrix contains a contradictory row, so the system is
                  inconsistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C5.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_1</m> is free, so the system is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C6.</term>
                <q>
                  There are two pivots.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-correctness-incomplete">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why Response C6 should not receive a two-digit correctness
            code under the current Episode Model.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-correctness-final-answer">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Identify one response in the preceding list that reaches an incorrect
            judgment using a true criterion, and one that uses a valid general
            inferential link with a false criterion. Explain why these are
            different errors.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART VII: GOAL COVERAGE                                       -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-goal">
      <title>Part VII: Evaluate Goal Coverage</title>

      <p>
        Practice Question A asks for four pieces of information:
        <ol>
          <li>whether the system is consistent;</li>
          <li>whether the solution is unique;</li>
          <li>which variables are basic; and</li>
          <li>which variables are free.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        For each response below:
        <ol>
          <li>identify every episode;</li>
          <li>evaluate the local justification;</li>
          <li>list which parts of the prompt are addressed; and</li>
          <li>
            classify goal coverage as complete, partial, or absent.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-goal-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G1.</term>
                <q>
                  There is no contradictory row, so the system is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G2.</term>
                <q>
                  Columns one and two contain pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and
                  <m>x_2</m> are basic. Column three does not contain a pivot, so
                  <m>x_3</m> is free. Since a free variable exists, the solution
                  is not unique. The system is consistent because there is no
                  contradictory row.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G3.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_3</m> is free, so the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G4.</term>
                <q>
                  The system is consistent and has infinitely many solutions.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G5.</term>
                <q>
                  There are two pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are free.
                  Therefore, the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-goal-quality">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare Responses G3 and G5. One contains a sound local episode but
            incomplete goal coverage. The other reaches a correct conclusion
            through an incorrect variable classification. Explain why the two
            responses must not receive the same analytic description.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART VIII: OOV AND CODEBOOK REVISION                          -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-oov">
      <title>Part VIII: Identify OOV and No-Match Cases</title>

      <p>
        Use the miniature practice codebook from Part V. For each response:
        <ol>
          <li>extract any identifiable episode;</li>
          <li>determine whether an existing practice code fits;</li>
          <li>
            distinguish unfamiliar wording from a genuinely new reasoning type;
          </li>
          <li>
            classify the case as an existing match, OOV wording, no match,
            incomplete, or unresolved; and
          </li>
          <li>recommend what a reviewer should do next.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-oov-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O1.</term>
                <q>
                  The third variable has room to move, so there cannot be only one
                  answer.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O2.</term>
                <q>
                  The solution set has one degree of freedom, so it is a line
                  rather than a point.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O3.</term>
                <q>
                  The bottom row does not break the system.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O4.</term>
                <q>
                  The third column is dependent on the first two, so the system
                  has infinitely many solutions.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O5.</term>
                <q>
                  The matrix is wider than it is tall, so this particular system
                  is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-oov-proposal">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Select one response that appears to require a vocabulary or codebook
            revision. Draft:
            <ol>
              <li>a proposed canonical term or code name;</li>
              <li>a one-sentence definition;</li>
              <li>an inclusion rule;</li>
              <li>an exclusion rule; and</li>
              <li>a reason the proposal should or should not be adopted.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART IX: REVIEWING TOOL OUTPUT                                -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-review">
      <title>Part IX: Review a Proposed Pipeline Analysis</title>

      <p>
        In these exercises, the pipeline has supplied a proposed analysis.
        Review the proposal rather than beginning from scratch.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-review-packet-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Original response.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              There are two pivots for three variables, so <m>x_3</m> is free
              and the solution is not unique.
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            <term>Pipeline proposal.</term>
            <ul>
              <li><term>Screening:</term> <c>INCLUDE</c></li>
              <li>
                <term>Episode 1:</term>
                two pivots
                <m>\longrightarrow</m>
                <m>x_3</m> is free
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Episode 2:</term>
                <m>x_3</m> is free
                <m>\longrightarrow</m>
                solution is not unique
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Candidate codes:</term>
                <c>PC2</c>, <c>PC3</c>, <c>PC4</c>
              </li>
              <li><term>Disposition:</term> standard review</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            Review every proposed component. Confirm, revise, or reject each
            decision and provide a rationale.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-review-packet-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Original response.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              The third variable can change, so there are multiple answers.
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            <term>Pipeline proposal.</term>
            <ul>
              <li><term>Screening:</term> <c>EXCLUDE</c></li>
              <li><term>Rationale:</term> no mathematical keyword found</li>
              <li><term>Episodes:</term> none</li>
              <li><term>Disposition:</term> escalate</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            Review the proposal. Explain why keyword absence is or is not an
            adequate screening rationale.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-review-anchoring">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Recode the two original responses without looking at the proposed
            pipeline outputs. Compare the unaided result with the review you made
            after seeing the proposals. Did the proposal influence any decision?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PART X: CALIBRATION                                           -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-calibration">
      <title>Part X: Independent Calibration</title>

      <p>
        Calibration is the process of checking whether coders apply the analytic
        rules consistently. Calibration is not achieved merely by discussing
        examples together. Coders must first analyze the same responses
        independently.
      </p>

      <p>
        The research lead will provide a calibration set containing a mixture of:
        <ul>
          <li>clear single-episode responses;</li>
          <li>blank and bare-answer responses;</li>
          <li>incomplete episodes;</li>
          <li>multi-episode responses;</li>
          <li>nested or chained reasoning;</li>
          <li>multiple-match cases;</li>
          <li>OOV and no-match cases;</li>
          <li>
            responses with a true criterion but invalid inferential link; and
          </li>
          <li>
            responses with sound local reasoning but incomplete goal coverage.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-first-pass">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Independently code every response in the supplied calibration set.
            For each response, record:
            <ol>
              <li>screening decision and rationale;</li>
              <li>number of episodes;</li>
              <li><m>C</m>, <m>I</m>, and <m>J</m> for each episode;</li>
              <li>relationship among episodes;</li>
              <li>candidate control-structure codes;</li>
              <li>criterion and link correctness, when applicable;</li>
              <li>goal coverage;</li>
              <li>OOV, no-match, or ambiguity flags;</li>
              <li>confidence or uncertainty notes; and</li>
              <li>recommended disposition.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-compare">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare the independent results. For every disagreement, identify the
            earliest point in the workflow at which the codings diverged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Assign one or more of the following disagreement types:
            <ul>
              <li><c>SCREEN</c>: screening decision;</li>
              <li><c>BOUNDARY</c>: number or boundary of episodes;</li>
              <li><c>CRITERION</c>: interpretation of <m>C</m>;</li>
              <li><c>LINK</c>: interpretation of <m>I</m>;</li>
              <li><c>JUDGMENT</c>: interpretation of <m>J</m>;</li>
              <li><c>RELATION</c>: chained, independent, or nested;</li>
              <li><c>MATCH</c>: candidate code assignment;</li>
              <li><c>CORRECTNESS</c>: criterion or link correctness;</li>
              <li><c>COVERAGE</c>: completeness relative to the prompt;</li>
              <li><c>VOCABULARY</c>: OOV or canonical-term decision;</li>
              <li><c>POLICY</c>: different coding conventions applied; or</li>
              <li><c>OTHER</c>: disagreement not captured above.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-adjudicate">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Adjudicate each disagreement by:
            <ol>
              <li>returning to the original question and response;</li>
              <li>stating the evidence for each interpretation;</li>
              <li>consulting the current guide and codebook;</li>
              <li>reaching agreement when the evidence supports a decision;</li>
              <li>
                preserving the case as unresolved when the evidence is
                insufficient; and
              </li>
              <li>
                recording whether the disagreement requires a revision to the
                coding materials.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-revise">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Select one recurring disagreement. Draft a revision to the relevant
            definition, policy, example, or exclusion rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test the proposed revision on:
            <ul>
              <li>the response that produced the disagreement;</li>
              <li>at least two similar responses; and</li>
              <li>at least one near-miss response that should not receive the same
              treatment.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            Record whether the revision:
            <ul>
              <li>resolves the original disagreement;</li>
              <li>creates a new ambiguity;</li>
              <li>changes any previously coded response; and</li>
              <li>should be adopted, revised further, or rejected.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- CALIBRATION RECORD                                            -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-record">
      <title>Calibration Record</title>

      <p>
        The calibration process should produce a durable record rather than only
        a verbal discussion. Use a table with at least the following fields.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-calibration-record">
        <title>Recommended fields for documenting a calibration disagreement.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="24%"/>
          <col width="76%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Field</cell>
            <cell>What to record</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Response identifier</cell>
            <cell>
              The stable practice or research identifier, not the student's name.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Decision level</cell>
            <cell>
              Screening, segmentation, component interpretation, matching,
              correctness, goal coverage, or disposition.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Coder A decision</cell>
            <cell>The first coder's independent result and rationale.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Coder B decision</cell>
            <cell>The second coder's independent result and rationale.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Evidence</cell>
            <cell>
              The exact wording, notation, or feature supporting each
              interpretation.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Disagreement type</cell>
            <cell>
              The category from the disagreement taxonomy.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Adjudicated decision</cell>
            <cell>
              The agreed result or a statement that the case remains unresolved.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Reason for decision</cell>
            <cell>
              A concise explanation tied to the question, response, and current
              coding rule.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Follow-up action</cell>
            <cell>
              No change, add an example, clarify a definition, revise a policy,
              propose a vocabulary term, or revisit previous responses.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- FINAL ONBOARDING TASK                                         -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-final">
      <title>Final Onboarding Task</title>

      <p>
        After completing the guided exercises, each student researcher will
        independently analyze a small onboarding set selected by the research
        lead. This set should not contain responses reserved for the formal
        validation study.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-final-onboarding">
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each response in the onboarding set, prepare a complete analysis
            packet containing:
            <ol>
              <li>the original question and response identifier;</li>
              <li>screening decision and rationale;</li>
              <li>episode sequence <m>E(x)</m>;</li>
              <li>
                criterion, inferential link, and judgment for each episode;
              </li>
              <li>evidence supporting each component;</li>
              <li>episode relationships and nesting-policy decisions;</li>
              <li>candidate codebook matches and rationales;</li>
              <li>correctness annotations for complete episodes;</li>
              <li>goal-coverage assessment;</li>
              <li>uncertainty, OOV, or no-match flags;</li>
              <li>recommended disposition; and</li>
              <li>a short reviewer note explaining the most difficult decision.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        The research lead will review the packets and identify:
        <ul>
          <li>decisions the student can already make independently;</li>
          <li>decisions requiring additional practice;</li>
          <li>coding rules that remain unclear; and</li>
          <li>
            response types that should be discussed before the student begins
            coding project data.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-readiness">
      <title>Readiness Checklist</title>

      <p>
        Before beginning independent work with project data, a student researcher
        should be able to:
        <ul>
          <li>
            distinguish a response-level episode from a claim about a complete
            conception;
          </li>
          <li>
            screen a response without confusing codability with correctness;
          </li>
          <li>identify explicit, supported, and missing episode components;</li>
          <li>distinguish operations from enacted controls;</li>
          <li>segment common chained and independent responses;</li>
          <li>apply the current nesting policy consistently;</li>
          <li>match episodes using definitions rather than isolated keywords;</li>
          <li>
            evaluate criterion correctness separately from inferential-link
            correctness;
          </li>
          <li>evaluate goal coverage separately from local justification;</li>
          <li>recognize OOV, no-match, and unresolved cases;</li>
          <li>write concise evidence-based rationales;</li>
          <li>record disagreement without treating it as failure;</li>
          <li>participate in adjudication using the original evidence; and</li>
          <li>
            recommend a clarification when a coding rule does not adequately
            resolve a case.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 8: GLOSSARY                                          -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-glossary">
    <title>Glossary</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This glossary defines the central theoretical, analytic, and
        computational terms used in the project. The definitions are written for
        new student researchers and describe how each term is used within this
        guide.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some terms come from the cK&#162; framework, while others were introduced
        by this project to support the analysis of short written responses. The
        source of a term matters:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <term>cK&#162; terms</term> describe the broader theoretical model of
            mathematical knowing;
          </li>
          <li>
            <term>Episode Model terms</term> describe the local justification
            extracted from one written response; and
          </li>
          <li>
            <term>pipeline terms</term> describe how the computational tool
            organizes and supports the analysis.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The glossary is divided into four groups:
        <ol>
          <li>theoretical terms from the cK&#162; framework;</li>
          <li>terms from the Episode Model;</li>
          <li>pipeline and codebook terms; and</li>
          <li>human-review and research-quality terms.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- THEORETICAL TERMS                                             -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-glossary-theory">
      <title>Theoretical Terms</title>

      <dl width="medium">
        <li xml:id="glossary-conception">
          <title>Conception</title>

          <p>
            In the cK&#162; framework, a situated form of mathematical knowing
            modeled by
            <md>
              (P,R,L,\Sigma).
            </md>
            A conception describes a relationship among a class of problems,
            available operators, a representation system, and a control
            structure.
          </p>

          <p>
            A conception is not simply a fixed belief stored inside a student.
            It describes knowing as it functions within particular mathematical
            situations.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-knowing">
          <title>Knowing</title>

          <p>
            A person's situated mathematical understanding as it functions in
            interaction with a problem environment. In the cK&#162; tradition,
            <term>knowing</term> is distinguished from publicly recognized
            mathematical <term>knowledge</term>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-problem-class">
          <title>Problem Class, <m>P</m></title>

          <p>
            The collection of problems or situations that a conception can
            successfully address. In the quadruplet <m>(P,R,L,\Sigma)</m>, the
            component <m>P</m> identifies the sphere of problems for which the
            conception is useful.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-operator">
          <title>Operator, <m>R</m></title>

          <p>
            A mathematical action used to work on a problem or transform a
            representation. Examples include row reducing a matrix,
            differentiating a function, substituting into an equation, or
            rearranging a symbolic expression.
          </p>

          <p>
            In Balacheff's notation, <m>R</m> denotes operators, not
            representations.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-representation-system">
          <title>Representation System, <m>L</m></title>

          <p>
            The linguistic, symbolic, graphical, or technological means through
            which mathematical actions, results, and feedback are expressed.
            Examples include equations, matrices, diagrams, written language,
            and mathematical software interfaces.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-control-structure">
          <title>Control Structure, <m>\Sigma</m></title>

          <p>
            The means used to make choices, evaluate actions, validate results,
            and decide whether a mathematical task has been completed.
          </p>

          <p>
            A control structure is broader than one criterion in one response.
            For example, a student may enact a free-variable control by
            recognizing that a free variable exists and using that fact to judge
            whether a solution is unique.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-operation">
          <title>Operation</title>

          <p>
            A mathematical action that creates or transforms an object. Examples
            include computing a derivative, row reducing a matrix, solving an
            equation, or constructing a parametric solution.
          </p>

          <p>
            The project distinguishes an operation from a control: an operation
            produces or transforms something, while a control evaluates a feature
            and supports a decision. The distinction can occasionally be
            ambiguous and is treated as a project coding convention.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-control">
          <title>Control</title>

          <p>
            An enacted evaluative move that interprets a feature of the current
            mathematical state and supports a judgment. For example, recognizing
            that a matrix has no contradictory row and concluding that the system
            is consistent is a control.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-question-frame">
          <title>Question-Level Analytic Frame, <m>K_q</m></title>

          <p>
            The project's question-specific version of the cK&#162; quadruplet:
            <md>
              K_q=(P_q,R_q,L_q,\Sigma_q).
            </md>
            It records the relevant problem context, operations,
            representations, and controls for a particular question <m>q</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The frame describes the mathematical context used to analyze the
            response. It is not treated as the student's complete conception.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the tool, the part of this frame that does concrete work is stored
            as the question's <term>working codebook</term>: the control
            structures in scope for the question, each bound to the
            criterion-to-judgment link that is valid for it. That stored
            selection, not a full per-question quadruplet, is what the matching
            and correctness stages consult.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- EPISODE MODEL TERMS                                           -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-glossary-episode">
      <title>Episode Model Terms</title>

      <dl width="medium">
        <li xml:id="glossary-episode-model">
          <title>Episode Model</title>

          <p>
            The project-developed framework for representing local pieces of
            justification in short written responses. It is motivated by the
            cK&#162; framework but is not itself part of Balacheff's original
            model.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-episode-sequence">
          <title>Episode Sequence, <m>E(x)</m></title>

          <p>
            The ordered collection of episodes extracted from a response
            <m>x</m>:
            <md>
              E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_n\rangle.
            </md>
            The order usually follows the development of the reasoning in the
            written response.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-episode">
          <title>Episode, <m>e_k</m></title>

          <p>
            A local piece of justification represented as
            <md>
              e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k),
            </md>
            where <m>C_k</m> is a criterion, <m>I_k</m> is an inferential link,
            and <m>J_k</m> is a judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            An episode is evidence visible in a response. It is not a complete
            model of the student's conception or thought process.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-criterion">
          <title>Criterion, <m>C_k</m></title>

          <p>
            The observation, fact, condition, intermediate result, or
            mathematical feature cited in an episode. Examples include:
            <ul>
              <li>a free variable exists;</li>
              <li>there are fewer pivots than variables;</li>
              <li>the derivative is positive; or</li>
              <li>the reduced matrix contains no contradictory row.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            A criterion is not identical to a control structure. It is the
            particular condition recognized when a broader control is enacted in
            a response.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-inferential-link">
          <title>Inferential Link, <m>I_k</m></title>

          <p>
            The mathematical relationship or reasoning rule used to connect a
            criterion to a judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the episode
            <md>
              \text{a free variable exists}
              \longrightarrow
              \text{the solution is not unique},
            </md>
            the inferential link is that a system with a free variable cannot
            have exactly one solution.
          </p>

          <p>
            The inferential link is not read verbatim from a response. The
            per-question set of valid links is the project's normative
            reference, stored in the working codebook (the
            <c>normative_link</c> field of <c>question_controls</c>); the second
            correctness digit grades a response's link against that reference. A
            schema comment glosses this field with the letter <q>L</q>, but in
            the Episode Model the inferential link is always <m>I</m>, never
            <m>L</m> (which denotes the representation system).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-judgment">
          <title>Judgment, <m>J_k</m></title>

          <p>
            The conclusion, classification, or decision reached in an episode.
            Examples include:
            <ul>
              <li>the system is consistent;</li>
              <li>the solution is not unique;</li>
              <li>the vector is not in the span; or</li>
              <li>the function is increasing.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-complete-episode">
          <title>Complete Episode</title>

          <p>
            An episode for which the response supplies enough evidence to
            identify a criterion, an inferential link, and a judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            The link need not be written as a formal theorem, but it must be
            supported without requiring the coder to add new mathematical
            reasoning.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-incomplete-episode">
          <title>Incomplete Episode</title>

          <p>
            An episode in which one or more of the criterion, inferential link,
            or judgment cannot be identified from the available evidence.
          </p>

          <p>
            An incomplete episode is preserved as partial evidence rather than
            repaired or forced into a complete correctness code.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-explicit">
          <title>Explicit Evidence</title>

          <p>
            Evidence stated directly in the student's words, symbols, diagram,
            or calculations.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-strongly-supported">
          <title>Strongly Supported Evidence</title>

          <p>
            A component that is not stated word for word but is identified
            clearly by the structure and mathematical content of the response
            without requiring the coder to add new reasoning.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-missing-ambiguous">
          <title>Missing or Ambiguous Evidence</title>

          <p>
            Evidence that is not sufficiently present to support a defensible
            interpretation. Identifying the component would require guessing,
            repairing, or selecting among several plausible meanings.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-paraphrasing">
          <title>Paraphrasing</title>

          <p>
            Restating the student's idea in clearer or standardized language
            without changing its mathematical content.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-interpretation">
          <title>Interpretation</title>

          <p>
            Identifying the mathematical role of a statement using evidence from
            the response and the question context.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-repair">
          <title>Repair</title>

          <p>
            Adding a missing condition, theorem, intermediate result, or
            conclusion so that the student's reasoning becomes mathematically
            complete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repair is not permitted in episode extraction. Missing reasoning
            must remain recorded as missing.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-segmentation">
          <title>Episode Segmentation</title>

          <p>
            The process of deciding how many episodes appear in a response and
            where the boundary between episodes occurs.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-chained-episodes">
          <title>Chained Episodes</title>

          <p>
            Episodes in which the judgment of one episode becomes the criterion
            for a later episode.
          </p>

          <p>
            For example:
            <md>
              \text{fewer pivots than variables}
              \longrightarrow
              \text{a free variable exists}
              \longrightarrow
              \text{the solution is not unique}.
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-independent-episodes">
          <title>Independent Episodes, <m>e_a\parallel e_b</m></title>

          <p>
            Separate justifications that do not depend on one another for coding
            purposes. A student might, for example, justify the same conclusion
            using both a pivot argument and a dimension argument.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-nested-episodes">
          <title>Nested Episodes, <m>e_i\sqsubseteq e_j</m></title>

          <p>
            A relationship in which one episode functions as a sub-step within a
            larger episode or argument.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nested episodes may also be chained. The terms describe related but
            not necessarily mutually exclusive features.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-nesting-policy">
          <title>Nesting Policy, <m>\pi</m></title>

          <p>
            The coding convention that determines how nested episodes are
            retained. A policy may direct coders to keep:
            <ul>
              <li>both the parent and child episodes;</li>
              <li>only the parent episode; or</li>
              <li>only the child episode.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            The research team fixes this policy before validation. The
            mathematics alone does not determine it.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-valid-link-set">
          <title>Valid-Inference Set, <m>\mathcal{T}</m></title>

          <p>
            The question-specific collection of inferential links regarded as
            mathematically valid for the task. An episode's inferential link is
            evaluated against this set rather than against a universal
            question-independent rule.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-normative-reference">
          <title>Normative Reference</title>

          <p>
            The agreed mathematical standard used to evaluate an episode. It
            includes:
            <ul>
              <li>the true mathematical state of the problem; and</li>
              <li>
                the question-specific inferential links that validly connect
                criteria to judgments.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-correctness-code">
          <title>Correctness Annotation</title>

          <p>
            The two-part annotation assigned to a complete episode:
            <md>
              \operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c_k^1,c_k^2).
            </md>
            The first digit records whether the criterion is true. The second
            records whether the inferential link validly supports the judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two-digit code is adapted from prior work (Gerami et&#160;al.,
            2024) and layered on top of the episode description. It is not part
            of the cK&#162; model, which does not ask whether a student's
            reasoning was right.
          </p>

          <p>
            The possible pairs are:
            <dl width="narrow">
              <li>
                <title><m>(1,1)</m></title>
                <p>True criterion and valid inferential link.</p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title><m>(1,0)</m></title>
                <p>True criterion but invalid inferential link.</p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title><m>(0,1)</m></title>
                <p>
                  False criterion, although the general criterion-to-judgment
                  relationship is valid.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title><m>(0,0)</m></title>
                <p>False criterion and invalid inferential link.</p>
              </li>
            </dl>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-criterion-correctness">
          <title>Criterion Correctness</title>

          <p>
            Whether the criterion stated in an episode is true in the actual
            mathematical situation. It is represented by the first digit
            <m>c_k^1</m> of the correctness annotation.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-link-correctness">
          <title>Inferential-Link Correctness</title>

          <p>
            Whether the criterion validly supports the judgment for the
            particular question. It is represented by the second digit
            <m>c_k^2</m> of the correctness annotation.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-justification-quality">
          <title>Justification Quality</title>

          <p>
            The mathematical quality of the local reasoning in an episode,
            including whether its criterion is true and its inferential link is
            valid.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-goal-coverage">
          <title>Goal Coverage</title>

          <p>
            The extent to which the response addresses all conclusions or tasks
            requested by the question.
          </p>

          <p>
            Goal coverage is evaluated separately from justification quality. A
            response may contain sound local episodes but fail to answer the
            complete prompt.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-response-level-evidence">
          <title>Response-Level Evidence</title>

          <p>
            Information visible in one student response that supports a limited
            interpretation of the student's reasoning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Response-level evidence is not treated as a complete model of what
            the student knows or believes across other situations.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- PIPELINE AND CODEBOOK TERMS                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-glossary-pipeline">
      <title>Pipeline and Codebook Terms</title>

      <dl width="medium">
        <li xml:id="glossary-pipeline">
          <title>Pipeline</title>

          <p>
            The ordered computational workflow that organizes and analyzes
            student responses:
            <md>
              \text{ingest}
              \longrightarrow
              \text{screen}
              \longrightarrow
              \text{extract}
              \longrightarrow
              \text{match}
              \longrightarrow
              \text{dispose}.
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            Human review surrounds the workflow and is not merely an additional
            automated stage.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-ingest">
          <title>Ingest</title>

          <p>
            The stage that reads a source data file and creates standardized,
            traceable records for student responses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ingest preserves the original response and its source information. It
            does not interpret or code the reasoning.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-screening">
          <title>Screening</title>

          <p>
            The process of deciding whether a response contains enough
            interpretable justification to continue through episode analysis.
          </p>

          <p>
            Screening concerns codability, not correctness. An incorrect
            response may be codable, while a correct bare answer may not be.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-include">
          <title><c>INCLUDE</c></title>

          <p>
            A screening decision indicating that the response contains enough
            evidence of mathematical justification to continue through the
            pipeline.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-exclude">
          <title><c>EXCLUDE</c></title>

          <p>
            A screening decision indicating that the response does not satisfy
            the current requirements for episode analysis.
          </p>

          <p>
            Excluded responses are preserved in the dataset together with the
            reason for exclusion.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-flag">
          <title>Review Flag</title>

          <p>
            An indicator that a response or decision requires human attention.
            A flag may be triggered by low confidence, ambiguity, incomplete
            reasoning, multiple matches, unfamiliar terminology, or another
            review rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the current screening stage, the review flag is realized as a
            distinct third label, <c>FLAG</c>, alongside <c>INCLUDE</c> and
            <c>EXCLUDE</c>. The screener assigns <c>FLAG</c> to borderline
            responses (e.g., work shown with no connector to a conclusion) and
            Stage 5 routes a flagged response to <c>escalate</c>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-extraction">
          <title>Extraction</title>

          <p>
            The stage that proposes one or more episodes from a written
            response. It identifies possible criteria, inferential links,
            judgments, episode boundaries, relationships, and missing
            components.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-chain">
          <title>Reasoning Chain</title>

          <p>
            The stored or displayed sequence of reasoning steps associated with
            a response. In the current project terminology, the reasoning chain
            is the implementation carrier of the response's episode sequence.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-chain-step">
          <title>Chain Step</title>

          <p>
            One stored step within a reasoning chain. In the implementation, a
            premise slot may carry the episode's criterion and a conclusion slot
            may carry its judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            A database chain step is a storage representation. The analytic
            object remains the episode <m>(C,I,J)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-controlled-vocabulary">
          <title>Controlled Vocabulary</title>

          <p>
            A project-approved collection of standardized terms used to
            represent recurring mathematical observations, conditions, and
            conclusions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The controlled vocabulary allows different surface expressions such
            as <q>one variable can be anything</q> and
            <q>a free variable exists</q> to be compared without erasing the
            student's original wording.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-canonical-form">
          <title>Canonical Form</title>

          <p>
            The standardized wording assigned to a mathematical idea in the
            controlled vocabulary. For example, several informal expressions may
            be normalized to a canonical term such as
            <c>free_var_exists</c>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-oov">
          <title>Out of Vocabulary, <c>OOV</c></title>

          <p>
            A flag indicating that a mathematically relevant idea in the
            response is not represented in the current controlled vocabulary.
          </p>

          <p>
            An OOV case should be preserved for review rather than forced into
            the nearest existing term.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-codebook">
          <title>Codebook</title>

          <p>
            A structured guide for classifying the mathematical reasoning found
            in responses. A codebook typically contains:
            <ul>
              <li>code identifiers and names;</li>
              <li>definitions;</li>
              <li>inclusion and exclusion rules;</li>
              <li>positive examples;</li>
              <li>near-miss examples;</li>
              <li>confusable codes; and</li>
              <li>question-specific information when needed.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-code">
          <title>Code</title>

          <p>
            A named category in the codebook used to describe an episode or
            control structure. A response may contain several episodes and may
            therefore receive more than one code.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-question-specific-codebook">
          <title>Question-Specific Coding Information</title>

          <p>
            The criteria, judgments, inferential links, examples, and exclusions
            that determine how the broader codebook applies to one particular
            question.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-match">
          <title>Match</title>

          <p>
            A proposed correspondence between an extracted episode and a
            codebook entry.
          </p>

          <p>
            A match is a candidate for human consideration, not automatically a
            final research code.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-candidate-code">
          <title>Candidate Code</title>

          <p>
            A codebook entry proposed as a plausible description of an episode.
            The pipeline may record zero, one, or several candidate codes.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-multiple-match">
          <title>Multiple Match</title>

          <p>
            A case in which more than one codebook entry plausibly describes an
            episode or response. A multiple-match case is routed to the
            <c>standard_review</c> disposition; it is a reason for review, not a
            disposition of its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multiple matches may indicate several genuine controls, overlapping
            code definitions, a nesting issue, or an ambiguous response.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-no-match">
          <title>No Match</title>

          <p>
            A case in which an episode can be extracted, but no current codebook
            entry adequately describes it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A no-match case is different from an OOV case: the vocabulary may be
            sufficient to represent the episode even though the codebook has no
            fitting classification.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-matching">
          <title>Matching</title>

          <p>
            The stage that compares extracted episodes with the current
            codebook and records every plausible candidate code, together with
            the supporting evidence and rationale.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-disposition">
          <title>Disposition</title>

          <p>
            The workflow decision that determines what happens to a response
            next. The pipeline records it as one of six values:
            <ul>
              <li><c>auto_accept</c>;</li>
              <li>
                <c>standard_review</c> (also where several plausible codes are
                sent);
              </li>
              <li>
                <c>problem_type_flag</c> (defined in the schema, not produced by
                the current baseline);
              </li>
              <li><c>terminology_flag</c>;</li>
              <li><c>no_match</c>; or</li>
              <li><c>escalate</c>.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-disposer">
          <title>Disposer</title>

          <p>
            The fifth pipeline component. It reads the structured outputs of
            screening, extraction, and matching and assigns a disposition.
          </p>

          <p>
            The disposer does not introduce new mathematical analysis.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-escalation">
          <title>Escalation</title>

          <p>
            A disposition that sends a response for more extensive human review
            because the ordinary workflow cannot resolve it reliably.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-rationale">
          <title>Rationale</title>

          <p>
            A brief explanation of why a screening decision, episode
            interpretation, candidate match, disposition, or human override was
            made.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful rationale refers to visible evidence and the current coding
            rule rather than speculating about what the student was thinking.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-confidence">
          <title>Confidence</title>

          <p>
            A recorded estimate of how certain the tool or reviewer is about a
            proposed result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confidence does not replace the decision itself. It may be used to
            prioritize cases for review or support a later flagging rule.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- HUMAN REVIEW AND RESEARCH QUALITY TERMS                       -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-glossary-review">
      <title>Human Review and Research-Quality Terms</title>

      <dl width="medium">
        <li xml:id="glossary-human-in-loop">
          <title>Human-in-the-Loop</title>

          <p>
            A workflow in which computational tools assist with analysis while
            human researchers retain authority over interpretive and
            mathematical decisions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The human role includes developing coding rules, reviewing uncertain
            cases, confirming or revising proposed analyses, and deciding when
            the framework itself needs revision.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-human-review">
          <title>Human Review</title>

          <p>
            The process by which a researcher inspects the original response,
            question, proposed episodes, candidate codes, correctness
            information, flags, and disposition.
          </p>

          <p>
            Human review is a standing layer across the pipeline rather than an
            isolated final stage.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-review-packet">
          <title>Review Packet</title>

          <p>
            The complete collection of information presented to a reviewer for
            one response. It may include:
            <ul>
              <li>the original question and response;</li>
              <li>screening output;</li>
              <li>proposed episodes;</li>
              <li>supporting evidence;</li>
              <li>candidate codes;</li>
              <li>correctness annotations;</li>
              <li>flags; and</li>
              <li>the proposed disposition.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-confirmation">
          <title>Confirmation</title>

          <p>
            A human-review decision accepting the proposed analysis without
            substantive change.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-override">
          <title>Override</title>

          <p>
            A human-review decision that changes or rejects a proposed screening
            label, episode, candidate code, correctness annotation, or
            disposition. On candidate codes, an override is recorded as one of a
            fixed set of edit kinds (<c>confirm</c>, <c>add</c>, <c>remove</c>,
            <c>replace</c>, or <c>reject_all</c>) and appended to an override log
            rather than overwriting the tool's original proposal.
          </p>

          <p>
            An override does not automatically mean that the tool failed. It may
            reveal ambiguity, overlapping code definitions, a missing vocabulary
            term, an inconsistent human decision, or a tool error.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-unresolved">
          <title>Unresolved Case</title>

          <p>
            A response or decision for which the available evidence and coding
            rules do not support one defensible final interpretation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preserving an unresolved case is preferable to creating artificial
            agreement.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-disagreement">
          <title>Disagreement</title>

          <p>
            A difference between coders, between a tool and a coder, or among
            codebook versions. Disagreements may concern screening, episode
            boundaries, component interpretation, matching, correctness, goal
            coverage, or policy.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-disagreement-type">
          <title>Disagreement Type</title>

          <p>
            A category identifying the earliest point at which two analyses
            diverge. Common types include:
            <ul>
              <li>screening disagreement;</li>
              <li>episode-boundary disagreement;</li>
              <li>criterion disagreement;</li>
              <li>inferential-link disagreement;</li>
              <li>judgment disagreement;</li>
              <li>matching disagreement;</li>
              <li>correctness disagreement;</li>
              <li>goal-coverage disagreement; and</li>
              <li>policy disagreement.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-adjudication">
          <title>Adjudication</title>

          <p>
            The structured process of reviewing a disagreement, comparing the
            evidence and coding rules, and reaching a documented decision when
            the evidence supports one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adjudication may also result in an unresolved case or a proposed
            revision to the coding materials.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-calibration">
          <title>Calibration</title>

          <p>
            The process of checking whether coders apply the analytic protocol
            consistently. Coders first analyze the same responses independently,
            compare their results, classify disagreements, and adjudicate them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The agreement between two independent coders, measured before
            adjudication, is summarized by a standard inter-rater reliability
            statistic such as Cohen's <m>\kappa</m> or Krippendorff's
            <m>\alpha</m>. That number is the human ceiling against which the
            tool is later compared, and it is one of the project's own research
            results.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-gold-standard">
          <title>Gold-Standard Dataset</title>

          <p>
            A collection of responses with carefully developed human annotations
            used to train coders, evaluate the pipeline, or compare different
            analytic methods.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gold-standard labels should be produced through an explicit coding
            and adjudication process rather than by one person's unreviewed
            judgment.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-coding-policy">
          <title>Coding Policy</title>

          <p>
            An agreed convention governing decisions that are not determined by
            the mathematics alone. Examples include:
            <ul>
              <li>screening requirements;</li>
              <li>nested-episode treatment;</li>
              <li>multiple-code rules;</li>
              <li>confidence thresholds; and</li>
              <li>handling of incomplete episodes.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-audit-trail">
          <title>Audit Trail</title>

          <p>
            The preserved record showing how an analytic result was produced.
            An audit trail may include the original response, intermediate
            pipeline outputs, rationales, model and prompt versions, codebook
            version, reviewer decisions, and later revisions.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-auditable">
          <title>Auditable</title>

          <p>
            Able to be inspected and reconstructed. An auditable result allows a
            researcher to determine what evidence, rules, tools, and human
            decisions produced it.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-provenance">
          <title>Provenance</title>

          <p>
            Information recording the origin and processing history of a
            response or analytic result. Provenance may include:
            <ul>
              <li>the source file;</li>
              <li>the question and response identifiers;</li>
              <li>the pipeline run;</li>
              <li>the codebook and vocabulary versions;</li>
              <li>the prompt and model versions; and</li>
              <li>the reviewer and review date.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-pipeline-run">
          <title>Pipeline Run</title>

          <p>
            One execution of the analysis pipeline under a specified
            configuration. A run is associated with particular codebook,
            vocabulary, prompt, model, and software versions.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-versioning">
          <title>Versioning</title>

          <p>
            The practice of preserving identifiable versions of codebooks,
            vocabularies, prompts, models, schemas, and software rather than
            silently replacing earlier forms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Versioning allows two configurations to be compared on the same
            responses and keeps older analyses interpretable.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-reproducible">
          <title>Reproducible</title>

          <p>
            Able to be repeated using the documented data, rules, versions, and
            procedures. Reproducibility does not guarantee that every
            interpretation is correct, but it makes the process transparent and
            testable.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-idempotent">
          <title>Idempotent Ingest</title>

          <p>
            An ingest process in which importing the same response again does
            not create a duplicate record. Stable identifiers and duplicate
            checks make repeated imports safe.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-stable-identifier">
          <title>Stable Identifier</title>

          <p>
            A persistent code used to refer to the same response, question,
            student, import, or pipeline run across stages and files.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stable identifiers allow records to be linked without relying on a
            student's name or the row number of a temporary spreadsheet.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-codability">
          <title>Codability</title>

          <p>
            Whether a response contains enough interpretable justification to
            be analyzed under the current protocol.
          </p>

          <p>
            Codability is different from mathematical correctness.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-final-answer-correctness">
          <title>Final-Answer Correctness</title>

          <p>
            Whether the response reaches the mathematically correct final
            conclusion requested by the question.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is different from episode-level correctness. A response can
            contain a correct final answer with no codable justification, or a
            correct local episode without complete goal coverage.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-keyword-matching">
          <title>Keyword Matching</title>

          <p>
            Identifying possible codes from words or phrases appearing in a
            response. Keywords may be useful clues, but they are not sufficient
            for final coding because the same word can play different
            mathematical roles.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="glossary-human-authority">
          <title>Human Authority</title>

          <p>
            The project principle that final interpretive decisions remain under
            human control. The tool may propose, organize, and flag, but it does
            not independently establish a research finding.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- NOTATION SUMMARY                                              -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-glossary-notation">
      <title>Notation Summary</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-glossary-notation">
        <title>Principal notation used in the guide.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="28%"/>
          <col width="72%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Notation</cell>
            <cell>Meaning</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>(P,R,L,\Sigma)</m></cell>
            <cell>
              A conception: problems, operators, representation system, and
              control structure.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>K_q=(P_q,R_q,L_q,\Sigma_q)</m></cell>
            <cell>The analytic frame for question <m>q</m>.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>E(x)=\langle e_1,\ldots,e_n\rangle</m></cell>
            <cell>The episode sequence extracted from response <m>x</m>.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k)</m></cell>
            <cell>
              Episode <m>k</m>: criterion, inferential link, and judgment.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>e_i\sqsubseteq e_j</m></cell>
            <cell>Episode <m>e_i</m> is nested within episode <m>e_j</m>.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>e_a\parallel e_b</m></cell>
            <cell>
              Episodes <m>e_a</m> and <m>e_b</m> are independent for coding
              purposes.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
            <cell>The project policy for coding nested episodes.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>\mathcal{T}</m></cell>
            <cell>
              The question-specific collection of valid inferential links.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>
              <m>\operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c_k^1,c_k^2)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              The correctness annotation: criterion correctness followed by
              inferential-link correctness.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- CLOSING SECTION                                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-next-steps">
    <title>Next Steps: Beginning Project Work</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Completing this guide is the beginning of the onboarding process, not
        the end. The next goal is to move from guided examples to independent
        analysis while preserving the habits introduced throughout the guide:
        careful attention to evidence, explicit rationales, consistent use of
        the coding rules, and honest documentation of uncertainty.
      </p>

      <p>
        New student researchers will not begin by coding a large collection of
        research responses. Instead, they will complete a short sequence of
        increasingly independent tasks:
        <md>
          \text{guided practice}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{independent calibration}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{adjudication}
          \longrightarrow
          \text{supervised project coding}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The purpose of this sequence is not to eliminate every disagreement.
        It is to ensure that disagreements can be located, explained, and used
        to improve the analytic method.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-overview">
      <title>The Onboarding Sequence</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-onboarding-sequence">
        <title>Recommended sequence for beginning project work.</title>

        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="18%"/>
          <col width="35%"/>
          <col width="47%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Step</cell>
            <cell>Student task</cell>
            <cell>Evidence of completion</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1. Orientation</cell>
            <cell>
              Read the guide and discuss the central terms with the research
              team.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              The student can explain the difference among a conception, a
              control structure, and a response-level episode.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2. Guided practice</cell>
            <cell>
              Complete selected exercises with access to the examples and
              glossary.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              The student can identify criteria, inferential links, judgments,
              incomplete episodes, and common human decision points.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3. Independent calibration</cell>
            <cell>
              Analyze a small common set without seeing another coder's
              decisions.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              A complete coding record containing decisions, evidence, rationales,
              and uncertainty notes.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>4. Comparison</cell>
            <cell>
              Compare independent analyses and classify each disagreement.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              A disagreement table identifying the earliest stage at which the
              analyses diverged.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>5. Adjudication</cell>
            <cell>
              Discuss disagreements using the question, response, codebook, and
              current coding policies.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              An adjudicated decision or an explicit unresolved-case record.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>6. Supervised coding</cell>
            <cell>
              Analyze a small batch of project responses and submit them for
              review.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Evidence that the student can apply the protocol independently and
              identify cases requiring escalation.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        This sequence runs alongside the rest of the book and the project's
        coding packet. Before independent coding, a new student researcher
        should have read the <xref ref="ch-automation-tool-roadmap" text="title"/>
        and the <xref ref="ch-pipeline-demo" text="title"/> for the concrete
        pipeline, the Analytic Framework section of
        <xref ref="ch-framing-models" text="title"/> for the formal Episode
        Model, and the project's <term>Coder's Handbook</term> for the working
        coding rules and coding-sheet columns. The
        <xref ref="ch-using-the-tool" text="title"/> shows the prototype a
        reviewer actually operates.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-orientation">
      <title>Step 1: Confirm the Core Ideas</title>

      <p>
        Before beginning independent coding, a student researcher should be able
        to explain the following ideas in their own words:
        <ul>
          <li>
            A single written response provides evidence about local reasoning but
            does not reveal a student's complete conception.
          </li>
          <li>
            An episode is represented as
            <m>e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k)</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Screening concerns whether a response is codable, not whether it is
            correct.
          </li>
          <li>
            Operations generate or transform mathematical objects, while controls
            evaluate information and support judgments.
          </li>
          <li>
            Criterion correctness and inferential-link correctness are evaluated
            separately.
          </li>
          <li>
            Goal coverage is separate from the quality of an individual episode.
          </li>
          <li>
            Candidate codes proposed by a tool are not automatically final
            research codes.
          </li>
          <li>
            Ambiguity, disagreement, OOV language, and incomplete reasoning must
            be preserved rather than forced into an existing category.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A student who cannot yet explain one of these distinctions should return
        to the relevant section before beginning the independent calibration
        task.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-calibration">
      <title>Step 2: Complete an Independent Calibration Set</title>

      <p>
        The research lead will provide a small set of synthetic or previously
        approved training responses. Each student researcher will analyze the
        same responses independently.
      </p>

      <p>
        For each response, record:
        <ol>
          <li>the response identifier;</li>
          <li>the screening decision and rationale;</li>
          <li>the proposed episode sequence <m>E(x)</m>;</li>
          <li>
            the criterion, inferential link, and judgment for each episode;
          </li>
          <li>
            whether each component is explicit, strongly supported, missing, or
            ambiguous;
          </li>
          <li>the relationship among multiple episodes;</li>
          <li>all plausible candidate codes;</li>
          <li>
            the correctness annotation for each complete episode;
          </li>
          <li>the goal-coverage assessment;</li>
          <li>any OOV, no-match, or uncertainty flags;</li>
          <li>the recommended disposition; and</li>
          <li>a brief evidence-based rationale for difficult decisions.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        During the independent first pass:
        <ul>
          <li>do not view another coder's work;</li>
          <li>do not change the codebook;</li>
          <li>do not invent a new policy for one difficult response;</li>
          <li>do not repair missing student reasoning; and</li>
          <li>
            mark uncertain cases rather than choosing an unsupported answer.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-compare">
      <title>Step 3: Compare the Independent Analyses</title>

      <p>
        After all coders have completed the calibration set, compare the results.
        Begin by locating the earliest point at which the analyses differ.
      </p>

      <p>
        A disagreement may concern:
        <ul>
          <li>screening;</li>
          <li>the number or boundaries of episodes;</li>
          <li>the criterion;</li>
          <li>the inferential link;</li>
          <li>the judgment;</li>
          <li>the relationship among episodes;</li>
          <li>the candidate codebook match;</li>
          <li>criterion or link correctness;</li>
          <li>goal coverage;</li>
          <li>OOV or no-match status;</li>
          <li>the nesting policy;</li>
          <li>the recommended disposition; or</li>
          <li>another coding-policy issue.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not begin by asking which coder is correct. First determine whether
        the coders:
        <ul>
          <li>noticed different evidence;</li>
          <li>interpreted the same evidence differently;</li>
          <li>applied different coding rules;</li>
          <li>used different mathematical reference information; or</li>
          <li>
            encountered a case that the current guide does not resolve.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-adjudication">
      <title>Step 4: Adjudicate and Improve the Guidance</title>

      <p>
        During adjudication, return to the original question and response.
        Compare each interpretation with the current codebook, controlled
        vocabulary, normative reference, and coding policies.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each disagreement should produce one of the following outcomes:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Confirmed decision</title>
            <p>
              The existing evidence and rules support one interpretation.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Clarified decision</title>
            <p>
              Agreement is reached after a definition, example, or policy is
              interpreted more carefully.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Guidance revision</title>
            <p>
              The case reveals that the codebook, vocabulary, or guide needs a
              clearer rule or example.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Unresolved case</title>
            <p>
              The available response does not support one defensible
              interpretation.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        A guidance revision should be tested against more than the response that
        motivated it. Before adopting the revision, apply it to:
        <ul>
          <li>the original disagreement case;</li>
          <li>at least two similar cases; and</li>
          <li>at least one near-miss case that should be treated differently.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        When a definition or policy changes, record the change and determine
        whether previously coded responses must be revisited.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-project-data">
      <title>Step 5: Begin Working with Project Data</title>

      <p>
        After calibration, the research lead may assign a small batch of
        de-identified project responses. The first batch should contain a mixture
        of clear and difficult cases and should be reviewed before a larger batch
        is assigned.
      </p>

      <p>
        On real project data, the first independent pass is deliberately
        code-free: you describe each criterion-to-judgment move in your own
        words and leave the code column blank, following the project's coding
        protocol, until the project introduces its own codes to you. The
        practice codes in this guide (<c>PC1</c>&#8211;<c>PC5</c>) are for
        training only and are never used on research data.
      </p>

      <p>
        For each assigned response, the student researcher should:
        <ol>
          <li>read the exact question and its normative reference;</li>
          <li>read the entire response before assigning any code;</li>
          <li>complete the analysis independently;</li>
          <li>record evidence and rationales at each decision point;</li>
          <li>preserve all plausible matches and uncertainty flags;</li>
          <li>identify cases requiring human review or escalation;</li>
          <li>submit the completed analysis for review; and</li>
          <li>
            revise the record only through the project's approved review
            procedure.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Students should not be expected to resolve every difficult response
        independently. Recognizing that a response requires escalation is itself
        a successful analytic decision.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-data-practices">
      <title>Working Responsibly with Student Data</title>

      <p>
        Project responses are research data. They must be handled only through
        the storage, access, and communication procedures approved by the
        research team and institution.
      </p>

      <p>
        Student researchers must:
        <ul>
          <li>use only approved project accounts, folders, and devices;</li>
          <li>refer to students by project identifiers rather than names;</li>
          <li>
            avoid copying responses into personal documents, messaging systems,
            or unapproved cloud services;
          </li>
          <li>
            avoid including identifying information in notes, presentations, or
            screenshots;
          </li>
          <li>
            report accidental disclosure or questionable data handling
            immediately; and
          </li>
          <li>
            follow all applicable IRB and institutional data-governance
            requirements.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Real student responses must not be submitted to an external language
        model, chatbot, or other third-party service unless the research lead has
        confirmed that the specific use and provider are approved. Synthetic
        responses should be used for prompt development, demonstrations, and
        unapproved external-tool testing.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-records">
      <title>Records to Preserve</title>

      <p>
        Student work contributes to the research only when the reasoning behind
        each decision is preserved. For every assigned batch, retain:
        <ul>
          <li>the response and question identifiers;</li>
          <li>
            the coding-rules (handbook), codebook, and vocabulary versions each
            record was coded under;
          </li>
          <li>the student's independent first-pass analysis;</li>
          <li>confidence and uncertainty notes;</li>
          <li>all disagreement classifications;</li>
          <li>the adjudicated result;</li>
          <li>the rationale for each override;</li>
          <li>any proposed changes to the coding materials; and</li>
          <li>the date and reviewer associated with each decision.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not replace an earlier interpretation by silently editing it. The
        project should preserve the original proposal and the later decision so
        that the change can be studied.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-communication">
      <title>Questions and Escalation</title>

      <p>
        Student researchers should bring forward cases that expose genuine
        analytic uncertainty. A useful question identifies:
        <ol>
          <li>the exact response and question;</li>
          <li>the decision that is difficult;</li>
          <li>the competing interpretations;</li>
          <li>the evidence supporting each interpretation;</li>
          <li>the relevant codebook or guide language; and</li>
          <li>the reason the current rule does not resolve the case.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        For example:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          I identified two possible episode segmentations. Under the first,
          <q>a free variable exists</q> is the judgment of the first episode and
          the criterion of the second. Under the second, the entire sentence is
          treated as one larger episode. The current nesting policy explains
          which episodes to retain after segmentation, but it does not clearly
          determine the boundary in this case.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This is more useful than stating only that
        <q>the response was confusing.</q>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-validation-boundary">
      <title>Before Formal Validation Begins</title>

      <p>
        Training and exploratory coding may reveal needed revisions to the
        analytic materials. These revisions should occur before the project
        begins a formal comparison of human and computational coding.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before producing a validation dataset, the research team should freeze:
        <ul>
          <li>the screening rules;</li>
          <li>the codebook version;</li>
          <li>the controlled-vocabulary version;</li>
          <li>the question-specific normative references;</li>
          <li>the valid-inference sets;</li>
          <li>the nesting policy <m>\pi</m>;</li>
          <li>the rules for incomplete episodes;</li>
          <li>the rules for multiple codes and OOV cases; and</li>
          <li>the adjudication procedure.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        If these materials change during validation, the project must record the
        new version and determine whether earlier responses need to be recoded.
        Otherwise, measured disagreement may reflect changing rules rather than
        differences among coders or between humans and the tool.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-readiness">
      <title>Readiness for Independent Coding</title>

      <p>
        A student researcher is ready to begin independent project coding when
        they can:
        <ul>
          <li>
            describe the project's claims without saying that one response
            reveals a complete conception;
          </li>
          <li>apply the screening policy without confusing codability and correctness;</li>
          <li>extract complete and incomplete episodes without repairing them;</li>
          <li>identify common chained, independent, and nested structures;</li>
          <li>apply the current nesting policy consistently;</li>
          <li>distinguish operations from enacted controls;</li>
          <li>
            match episodes using definitions and inferential relationships rather
            than keywords alone;
          </li>
          <li>
            evaluate criterion correctness and inferential-link correctness
            separately;
          </li>
          <li>evaluate goal coverage separately from local justification;</li>
          <li>recognize OOV, no-match, and unresolved cases;</li>
          <li>write concise evidence-based rationales;</li>
          <li>protect research data appropriately;</li>
          <li>work independently before comparison; and</li>
          <li>
            escalate difficult cases with a clear description of the competing
            interpretations.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Readiness does not mean that the student will agree with every other
        coder. It means that the student can apply the current protocol
        independently, explain each decision, and identify the source of
        uncertainty when the protocol does not yield a clear answer.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-next-steps-closing">
      <title>The Student Researcher's Contribution</title>

      <p>
        Student researchers contribute more than labor to this project. Their
        independent analyses help determine whether the coding framework is
        understandable, whether the codebook can be applied consistently, and
        where the computational pipeline requires human judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Careful disagreement is particularly valuable. A well-documented
        disagreement may reveal:
        <ul>
          <li>an ambiguous response;</li>
          <li>an unclear episode boundary;</li>
          <li>an overlapping code definition;</li>
          <li>a missing vocabulary term;</li>
          <li>an unstated question-specific assumption;</li>
          <li>an inconsistency in the coding policy; or</li>
          <li>a recurring error in the computational tool.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The aim is therefore not to assign labels as quickly as possible. It is
        to produce an analysis that is mathematically defensible, transparent,
        reproducible, and useful for understanding how mathematical
        justifications appear in student writing.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

</chapter>